// Question bank b4: exam-style questions inspired by a community SAA-C03 set (rewritten, answers re-checked).
SAA.addBank([
  {
    id: "b4-001", mod: 10, domain: "secure", type: "mcq",
    q: `A freight broker runs an SFTP service on two Amazon EC2 instances that have Elastic IP addresses. Partners connect from a fixed list of public IP addresses. The instances share a file system, and each partner is a Linux user with access to its own home directory. The company wants a serverless replacement that keeps shared storage with high IOPS, lets it restrict access to the partner IP addresses, and keeps per-user directory control. Which solution meets these requirements?`,
    options: [
      { t: `Create an AWS Transfer Family SFTP server with an internet-facing VPC endpoint that uses Elastic IP addresses and a security group allowing only partner IPs. Store files on Amazon EFS and use service-managed users with POSIX profiles.`, ok: true, why: `Transfer Family is serverless, a VPC endpoint supports Elastic IPs and security groups, and EFS gives shared high-IOPS storage with POSIX permissions per user.` },
      { t: `Create an AWS Transfer Family SFTP server with a publicly accessible endpoint that stores files in Amazon S3. Add an S3 bucket policy with an aws:SourceIp condition that lists the partner IP ranges.`, ok: false, why: `A public endpoint can't use a security group, and S3 sees requests from the Transfer Family service, not from the partners' IPs, so the condition doesn't filter partners.` },
      { t: `Move the shared storage to Amazon EFS. Run the SFTP software on EC2 instances in an Auto Scaling group behind a Network Load Balancer, and limit access with the instances' security group.`, ok: false, why: `It still runs and patches EC2 SFTP servers, so it isn't serverless.` },
      { t: `Create an AWS Transfer Family SFTP server with a VPC-hosted endpoint set to internal access only. Use an Amazon EFS file system with provisioned throughput for storage, create service-managed users with POSIX profiles, and require each partner to reach the endpoint through its own AWS Site-to-Site VPN connection.`, ok: false, why: `Partners connect over the internet today. Forcing a VPN on every partner adds work for them and isn't needed to restrict source IPs.` },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "b4-002", mod: 8, domain: "performance", type: "mcq",
    q: `A fintech company serves several fraud-scoring models to hundreds of internal teams through an asynchronous API. Each model is a container that downloads about 2 GB of model data from Amazon S3 at startup and keeps it in memory. Some models sit idle for weeks, and others receive bursts of thousands of requests at once. Callers say where results should be delivered. Which design should a solutions architect recommend to meet these requirements?`,
    options: [
      { t: `Send API requests to an Amazon SQS queue per model. Run each model as an Amazon ECS service that polls its queue, and scale the service tasks and the cluster capacity on queue depth.`, ok: true, why: `The queue absorbs bursts and decouples callers, and scaling ECS on backlog lets idle models drop to few or zero tasks while busy ones scale out.` },
      { t: `Put each model behind an Application Load Balancer with an Auto Scaling group of EC2 instances that scales on average CPU utilization, and have the API call the load balancer synchronously and wait for each result.`, ok: false, why: `The API is asynchronous and bursts would hit the instances directly. CPU-based scaling reacts late and keeps idle models running.` },
      { t: `Package each model as an AWS Lambda function invoked synchronously by the API. Load the 2 GB of model data from S3 on every invocation.`, ok: false, why: `Reloading 2 GB per request adds large latency and cost, and synchronous calls don't fit the asynchronous API.` },
      { t: `Publish requests to an Amazon SNS topic. Subscribe an EC2 instance per model that runs all the time and processes messages as they arrive.`, ok: false, why: `Always-on instances waste money on idle models and one instance per model can't absorb bursts of thousands of requests.` },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "b4-003", mod: 14, domain: "cost", type: "mcq",
    q: `A ticketing platform runs on EC2 On-Demand Instances. Its web nodes must run 24/7 at a steady size. Its worker nodes process queued jobs for a few hours a day, the count changes with load, and any job can be retried if a worker disappears. The company wants Auto Scaling to add and remove workers as load changes. Which purchasing approach meets these requirements <strong>MOST</strong> cost-effectively?`,
    options: [
      { t: `Use Reserved Instances or a Savings Plan for the web nodes and Spot Instances for the worker nodes.`, ok: true, why: `A commitment discounts the always-on web tier, and Spot is the cheapest option for retryable, short-lived workers.` },
      { t: `Use Spot Instances for the web nodes and buy Reserved Instances for the worker nodes to cover their busiest hours.`, ok: false, why: `Web nodes can't tolerate interruptions, and reserving capacity for workers that run a few hours a day wastes the commitment.` },
      { t: `Use Reserved Instances for both the web nodes and the peak number of worker nodes.`, ok: false, why: `Reserving peak worker capacity pays for instances that sit unused most of the day.` },
      { t: `Use Dedicated Hosts for the web nodes and On-Demand Instances for the worker nodes.`, ok: false, why: `Dedicated Hosts cost more and aren't needed, and On-Demand workers miss the Spot discount.` },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "b4-004", mod: 5, domain: "cost", type: "mcq",
    q: `A research lab is moving an on-premises database server to Amazon EC2. The database needs 6 TB of block storage, and monitoring shows peak load never goes above 12,000 IOPS. The lab wants to set disk performance separately from storage size so it doesn't have to overprovision capacity to get IOPS. Which Amazon EBS volume type meets these requirements <strong>MOST</strong> cost-effectively?`,
    options: [
      { t: `General Purpose SSD (gp3)`, ok: true, why: `gp3 lets you provision IOPS and throughput independently of size, up to 16,000 IOPS, at a lower price than io-class volumes.` },
      { t: `Provisioned IOPS SSD (io2 Block Express)`, ok: false, why: `It also decouples IOPS from size but costs much more per GB and per IOPS than gp3 for a 12,000 IOPS workload.` },
      { t: `General Purpose SSD (gp2)`, ok: false, why: `gp2 ties IOPS to volume size (3 IOPS per GB), so performance can't be set on its own.` },
      { t: `Throughput Optimized HDD (st1)`, ok: false, why: `st1 is built for large sequential throughput, not thousands of small random IOPS for a database.` },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "b4-005", mod: 12, domain: "secure", type: "mcq",
    q: `A clinic network keeps patient records on on-premises file servers that are almost full. Files change many times a day. A new regulation requires the company to log every read and write of each stored object. The company wants to move the existing data to AWS securely, keep using the files from on premises, and satisfy the audit rule. Which solution meets these requirements?`,
    options: [
      { t: `Deploy an AWS Storage Gateway S3 File Gateway to move the data into Amazon S3, and turn on AWS CloudTrail data events for the bucket.`, ok: true, why: `File Gateway keeps local access while storing data in S3, and CloudTrail data events record object-level reads and writes.` },
      { t: `Deploy an AWS Storage Gateway S3 File Gateway to move the data into Amazon S3, and rely on AWS CloudTrail management events for the bucket.`, ok: false, why: `Management events only cover bucket-level API calls. They don't log object reads and writes.` },
      { t: `Use AWS DataSync to copy the data to Amazon S3 once, and turn on AWS Config rules for the bucket to track changes.`, ok: false, why: `Config tracks resource configuration, not who read or wrote each object, and a one-time copy doesn't keep on-premises access.` },
      { t: `Use AWS Snowball Edge to ship the data to Amazon S3, and enable Amazon S3 Inventory reports on the bucket.`, ok: false, why: `Inventory lists objects but logs no access, and a shipped copy goes stale while files keep changing.` },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "b4-006", mod: 8, domain: "resilient", type: "mcq",
    q: `A logistics company has a Java web application that must run on Apache Tomcat and uses a MySQL database. The team has little AWS experience and wants the application to be highly available across Availability Zones, with deployments that don't take the whole site down. Which solution meets these requirements with the <strong>LEAST</strong> operational overhead?`,
    options: [
      { t: `Deploy the application with AWS Elastic Beanstalk in a load-balanced, multi-AZ Tomcat environment with a rolling deployment policy, and use Amazon RDS for MySQL with Multi-AZ.`, ok: true, why: `Beanstalk manages the Tomcat platform, load balancer, Auto Scaling, and rolling deploys, and RDS Multi-AZ covers the database.` },
      { t: `Build a golden AMI with Tomcat, run it in an Auto Scaling group across two Availability Zones behind an Application Load Balancer, use RDS for MySQL Multi-AZ, and write custom scripts to roll out new versions.`, ok: false, why: `It can be highly available, but building AMIs and deployment scripts is more work than a managed platform.` },
      { t: `Run Tomcat in a container on a single Amazon Lightsail instance with a managed MySQL database.`, ok: false, why: `One instance is a single point of failure, so it isn't highly available.` },
      { t: `Deploy the application to AWS Lambda behind Amazon API Gateway, and store data in Amazon DynamoDB.`, ok: false, why: `It needs a full rewrite away from Tomcat and MySQL, which is much more effort.` },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "b4-007", mod: 11, domain: "secure", type: "mcq",
    q: `A startup builds a serverless order API with Amazon API Gateway and AWS Lambda. One function must read and write items in a single Amazon DynamoDB table named Orders. The security team bans long-term credentials in code or configuration. Which solution gives the function access to the table in the <strong>MOST</strong> secure way?`,
    options: [
      { t: `Create an IAM role that trusts the Lambda service, attach a policy that allows only the needed read and write actions on the Orders table, and set it as the function's execution role.`, ok: true, why: `The execution role gives temporary credentials automatically and the policy is scoped to one table and the needed actions.` },
      { t: `Create an IAM user with DynamoDB access, store its access keys in Lambda environment variables, and read them at startup.`, ok: false, why: `It uses long-term keys, which the team banned, and keys in environment variables can leak.` },
      { t: `Create an IAM user that has a policy scoped to the Orders table, store the user's access keys in AWS Secrets Manager with automatic rotation, and have the function fetch the keys at the start of each invocation.`, ok: false, why: `Secrets Manager protects the keys, but they are still long-term user credentials. A role avoids them entirely.` },
      { t: `Attach the AmazonDynamoDBFullAccess managed policy to an IAM role that trusts the Lambda service, and set it as the execution role.`, ok: false, why: `It avoids long-term keys but grants every DynamoDB action on every table, which breaks least privilege.` },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "b4-008", mod: 8, domain: "cost", type: "multi",
    q: `A solar farm operator has inverters that upload .csv readings to an Amazon S3 bucket. Each file must be turned into a chart image as soon as it arrives for a live dashboard. Images are useless after 30 days. The .csv files must be kept for model training twice a year, and those training runs are scheduled weeks in advance. Which combination of steps meets these requirements <strong>MOST</strong> cost-effectively? (Choose two.)`,
    options: [
      { t: `Trigger an AWS Lambda function from S3 event notifications to convert each new .csv file into an image and write it back to the bucket.`, ok: true, why: `Lambda runs per upload with no idle cost and gives near-immediate images.` },
      { t: `Add S3 Lifecycle rules that move .csv files to S3 Glacier Deep Archive soon after upload and expire image files after 30 days.`, ok: true, why: `Training is planned weeks ahead, so a 12-hour restore is fine, and expiring images stops paying for them.` },
      { t: `Run an EC2 instance with a cron job every hour that converts new .csv files and uploads the images.`, ok: false, why: `It delays images by up to an hour and pays for an instance that is mostly idle.` },
      { t: `Add S3 Lifecycle rules that move .csv files to S3 Standard-IA after 30 days and move images to S3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval after 30 days.`, ok: false, why: `Standard-IA costs much more than Deep Archive for data read twice a year, and archiving images still pays for useless data.` },
      { t: `Run an AWS Batch job every night that converts the day's .csv files into images in one run.`, ok: false, why: `Nightly batches miss the "as soon as possible" requirement for the dashboard.` },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "b4-009", mod: 9, domain: "performance", type: "mcq",
    q: `An esports company runs a three-tier online game in a VPC with Amazon RDS for MySQL as its database. Thousands of players compete at once. The company wants to show a top-20 leaderboard that updates in near real time, and it must be able to pause a tournament and later resume it with the current scores intact. Which solution meets these requirements?`,
    options: [
      { t: `Use Amazon ElastiCache for Redis OSS with persistence and backups enabled, and keep scores in a sorted set that the web tier reads for the leaderboard.`, ok: true, why: `Redis sorted sets rank scores in memory instantly, and snapshots let the company restore the scores after a pause.` },
      { t: `Use Amazon ElastiCache for Memcached across multiple nodes to cache the result of a leaderboard query that runs against the RDS database every few seconds, and refresh the cache on a timer.`, ok: false, why: `Memcached has no persistence or sorted sets, so scores can't be restored after a pause and RDS still does the ranking work.` },
      { t: `Add an RDS read replica and run an ORDER BY query on the scores table each time the leaderboard is shown.`, ok: false, why: `Sorting thousands of rows on every page view is slower than an in-memory ranking and loads the database.` },
      { t: `Put Amazon CloudFront in front of the web tier and cache the leaderboard page for 60 seconds.`, ok: false, why: `A cached page is up to a minute stale, which isn't near real time, and it doesn't handle pausing and restoring scores.` },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "b4-010", mod: 13, domain: "performance", type: "mcq",
    q: `An online retailer wants to build and train machine learning models on customer purchase data to forecast demand and spot trends. Business analysts must see the model output directly in interactive business intelligence dashboards. The data science team is small and doesn't want to manage servers or ML infrastructure. Which solution meets these requirements with the <strong>LEAST</strong> operational overhead?`,
    options: [
      { t: `Use Amazon SageMaker to build and train the models, and use Amazon QuickSight to visualize the results.`, ok: true, why: `Both are fully managed: SageMaker handles training and hosting, QuickSight gives dashboards with no servers to run.` },
      { t: `Train the models on a self-managed Jupyter server on EC2, and build dashboards with Grafana on another EC2 instance.`, ok: false, why: `It works but the team must run and patch both servers.` },
      { t: `Use Amazon SageMaker to train the models, and send the results to Amazon OpenSearch Service with OpenSearch Dashboards for the analysts.`, ok: false, why: `OpenSearch is built for search and log analytics. Running a domain for BI dashboards is more overhead than QuickSight.` },
      { t: `Train the models with Apache Spark MLlib on an Amazon EMR cluster, and export results to spreadsheets for the analysts.`, ok: false, why: `Running EMR clusters is more overhead, and spreadsheets aren't interactive BI dashboards.` },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "b4-011", mod: 12, domain: "secure", type: "mcq",
    q: `A company runs production and test workloads in many AWS accounts that belong to one organization in AWS Organizations. Finance relies on a CostCenter tag for chargeback. Recently, engineers changed CostCenter values on resources, which broke reports. The company must stop anyone except a dedicated FinOps role from changing or removing the CostCenter tag in every account. Which solution meets these requirements?`,
    options: [
      { t: `Create a service control policy that denies tag changes to the CostCenter key for every principal except the FinOps role, and attach it to the organization root.`, ok: true, why: `An SCP sets a guardrail across all accounts that even admins can't override, and a condition can exempt the FinOps role.` },
      { t: `Create a tag policy that defines CostCenter as a required key with a list of allowed values, turn on enforcement for tagging operations, and attach the policy to the organization root.`, ok: false, why: `Tag policies standardize keys and values, but they don't stop a user from changing a tag to another allowed value or deleting it.` },
      { t: `Create an AWS Config rule that flags resources whose CostCenter tag changed and notify the finance team.`, ok: false, why: `Config detects the change after it happens. It doesn't prevent it.` },
      { t: `Attach an IAM policy that denies tagging actions to every IAM user and role in each account.`, ok: false, why: `Account admins could remove or bypass the policy, and maintaining it in every account is manual work.` },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "b4-012", mod: 14, domain: "resilient", type: "mcq",
    q: `A booking site runs on EC2 instances in an Auto Scaling group behind an Application Load Balancer in us-east-1 and stores data in an Amazon DynamoDB table. Leadership wants the site to keep running in us-west-2 if us-east-1 becomes unavailable, with as little downtime as possible during a failover. Which solution meets these requirements with the <strong>LEAST</strong> downtime?`,
    options: [
      { t: `Convert the table to a DynamoDB global table with a replica in us-west-2. Run the web tier in us-west-2 behind its own load balancer, and use Route 53 failover routing with health checks.`, ok: true, why: `The data is already replicated and the second stack is running, so Route 53 just shifts traffic when the health check fails.` },
      { t: `Schedule DynamoDB on-demand backups every hour and copy them to us-west-2 with AWS Backup. During an outage, restore the latest backup to a new table and launch the web stack in us-west-2 with AWS CloudFormation.`, ok: false, why: `Restoring a table and building the stack during an outage takes much longer and loses changes since the last backup.` },
      { t: `Export the table to Amazon S3 every hour, replicate the bucket to us-west-2, and import it into a new table when needed.`, ok: false, why: `Importing creates a new table at failover time, which adds a lot of downtime and up to an hour of data loss.` },
      { t: `Keep an AMI copy in us-west-2 and create an Auto Scaling group there with a capacity of zero. Point it at the us-east-1 DynamoDB table during a failover.`, ok: false, why: `If us-east-1 is down, the table there is unreachable, so the second Region can't serve data.` },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "b4-013", mod: 10, domain: "cost", type: "mcq",
    q: `A retailer must move a 15 TB on-premises MySQL database to Amazon RDS for MySQL within 3 weeks with minimal downtime. The data center already has a 1 Gbps AWS Site-to-Site VPN with plenty of spare capacity, and the database keeps taking writes during the move. Which solution migrates the database <strong>MOST</strong> cost-effectively?`,
    options: [
      { t: `Use AWS DMS over the existing VPN to run a full load into Amazon RDS for MySQL with ongoing change data capture, then cut over when replication catches up.`, ok: true, why: `15 TB over 1 Gbps takes a few days, DMS keeps changes in sync, and no new hardware or links are needed.` },
      { t: `Order a new 10 Gbps AWS Direct Connect connection, then use AWS DMS over it with ongoing replication.`, ok: false, why: `A new Direct Connect link costs more and can take weeks to provision. The existing VPN is fast enough.` },
      { t: `Take a mysqldump of the database, stop the application, upload the dump over the VPN, and import it into RDS.`, ok: false, why: `The application is down for the whole dump, upload, and import of 15 TB, which isn't minimal downtime.` },
      { t: `Order several AWS Snowball Edge Storage Optimized devices, copy the nightly database backup files onto them, ship them to AWS, and restore the backups into Amazon RDS for MySQL when the data lands in S3.`, ok: false, why: `Shipping adds days and cost, and the restored copy misses all writes made while the devices were in transit.` },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "b4-014", mod: 6, domain: "cost", type: "mcq",
    q: `A company migrated its PostgreSQL database to a single Amazon RDS for PostgreSQL DB instance. After a successful product launch, the database is constantly near its CPU and memory limits. The workload is steady around the clock and expected to stay that way for years. The company doesn't want to add more database instances. Which solution meets these requirements <strong>MOST</strong> cost-effectively?`,
    options: [
      { t: `Move the DB instance to a larger instance class and buy reserved DB instances that cover the new size.`, ok: true, why: `Scaling up handles the load without new instances, and a reservation cuts the cost of a steady multi-year workload.` },
      { t: `Move the DB instance to a larger instance class and keep paying On-Demand rates.`, ok: false, why: `It handles the load but misses the large discount that reservations give a steady workload.` },
      { t: `Add two read replicas and buy reserved DB instances for them.`, ok: false, why: `It adds instances, which the company doesn't want, and replicas don't help a write-heavy load.` },
      { t: `Migrate the database to Amazon Aurora Serverless v2 and let it scale capacity up and down automatically with the load.`, ok: false, why: `Serverless suits spiky loads. For a constant high load, a reserved provisioned instance is cheaper.` },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "b4-015", mod: 11, domain: "secure", type: "mcq",
    q: `An online store runs on EC2 instances behind an Application Load Balancer. Bots with constantly changing IP addresses are sending floods of requests to the checkout page, slowing the site for real shoppers. The security team fears a layer 7 DDoS. The company needs to block the abusive traffic while affecting legitimate users as little as possible. What should a solutions architect recommend to meet these requirements?`,
    options: [
      { t: `Associate an AWS WAF web ACL with the load balancer and add a rate-based rule that blocks clients that exceed a request threshold.`, ok: true, why: `Rate-based rules track request counts per IP automatically, so new bot IPs get blocked without hurting normal shoppers.` },
      { t: `Add deny rules to the network ACLs of the load balancer subnets for each abusive IP address that shows up in the ALB access logs, and review the logs every hour.`, ok: false, why: `The IPs keep changing, and NACLs have a small rule limit, so manual blocking can't keep up.` },
      { t: `Subscribe to AWS Shield Standard and enable it on the load balancer.`, ok: false, why: `Shield Standard is already on for everyone and covers layer 3/4 attacks, not floods of valid HTTP requests.` },
      { t: `Raise the Auto Scaling group's maximum size so the application can absorb the extra requests.`, ok: false, why: `It pays to serve the bots instead of blocking them, and cost grows with the attack.` },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "b4-016", mod: 11, domain: "secure", type: "mcq",
    q: `An accounting firm stores ledgers in an Amazon RDS for PostgreSQL DB instance in a private subnet. The DB instance is encrypted with a customer managed AWS KMS key. An external auditor, who has its own AWS account, needs its own copy of the database. The firm must not open any network path to its VPC. What is the <strong>MOST</strong> secure way to share the data with the auditor?`,
    options: [
      { t: `Create a snapshot encrypted with the customer managed key, share the snapshot with the auditor's account, and grant that account permission to use the key in the key policy.`, ok: true, why: `The data stays encrypted, nothing in the VPC is exposed, and access is limited to the auditor's account.` },
      { t: `Create an unencrypted copy of the snapshot and share it publicly so the auditor can restore it.`, ok: false, why: `A public snapshot exposes the ledgers to every AWS account.` },
      { t: `Create a read replica in a public subnet and give the auditor a database user and password.`, ok: false, why: `It opens a network path into the firm's environment, which the firm doesn't allow.` },
      { t: `Export the database tables to CSV files in an S3 bucket encrypted with SSE-KMS, and send the auditor a presigned URL that expires after seven days so it can download the files.`, ok: false, why: `The auditor wanted a database copy, and a URL can be forwarded to anyone until it expires.` },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "b4-017", mod: 3, domain: "performance", type: "mcq",
    q: `A media company created a VPC with a 10.0.0.0/24 CIDR block for a pilot. The workload grew, and the subnets have nearly run out of IP addresses for new EC2 instances. The company wants to keep the existing instances where they are and add capacity for future workloads. Which solution resolves this issue with the <strong>LEAST</strong> operational overhead?`,
    options: [
      { t: `Add a secondary IPv4 CIDR block to the VPC, create new subnets from it, and launch new resources there.`, ok: true, why: `A VPC can have extra CIDR blocks, so new subnets get more addresses without moving anything.` },
      { t: `Create a second VPC with a larger CIDR block, connect the two VPCs with VPC peering, and launch new workloads in the second VPC.`, ok: false, why: `It works but adds a VPC, peering, route updates, and security rules to manage.` },
      { t: `Create a new VPC with a larger CIDR block and migrate every instance into it.`, ok: false, why: `Moving every instance is a lot of work and causes disruption.` },
      { t: `Change the VPC's primary CIDR block from /24 to /16.`, ok: false, why: `The primary CIDR block of a VPC can't be changed after creation.` },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "b4-018", mod: 10, domain: "resilient", type: "multi",
    q: `A QA team used an Amazon RDS for MySQL DB instance for a test cycle. Before deleting it, the team made a logical dump with mysqldump and also kept the final DB snapshot that RDS took on deletion. For the next cycle, the team wants a new Amazon Aurora MySQL-Compatible DB cluster loaded with that data. Which solutions meet these requirements? (Choose two.)`,
    options: [
      { t: `Restore the RDS for MySQL final snapshot as a new Aurora MySQL DB cluster.`, ok: true, why: `Aurora MySQL can be created directly from an RDS for MySQL snapshot of a compatible version.` },
      { t: `Create an empty Aurora MySQL DB cluster and replay the mysqldump file into it with the mysql client from an EC2 instance.`, ok: true, why: `A mysqldump file is SQL, so it can be loaded into any MySQL-compatible database with the mysql client.` },
      { t: `Upload the mysqldump file to Amazon S3 and use the Aurora "restore from Amazon S3" feature to create the cluster.`, ok: false, why: `Aurora's S3 restore needs a physical Percona XtraBackup backup, not a logical mysqldump file.` },
      { t: `Use AWS DMS to read the final snapshot as a source endpoint and load it into Aurora.`, ok: false, why: `DMS reads from a running database, not from a snapshot.` },
      { t: `Use AWS Database Migration Service with AWS SCT to convert the snapshot schema before restoring it to Aurora.`, ok: false, why: `SCT is for converting between different engines. MySQL to Aurora MySQL needs no conversion.` },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "b4-019", mod: 9, domain: "cost", type: "mcq",
    q: `A travel blog runs on EC2 instances behind an Application Load Balancer in an Auto Scaling group across multiple Availability Zones. Most traffic is for static images, CSS, and JavaScript files. During traffic spikes, the Auto Scaling group launches many extra On-Demand Instances just to serve these files. Which redesign reduces cost <strong>MOST</strong> effectively?`,
    options: [
      { t: `Move the static files to an Amazon S3 bucket and serve them through an Amazon CloudFront distribution.`, ok: true, why: `CloudFront caches files at the edge and S3 serves them without servers, so the web fleet stops scaling for static content.` },
      { t: `Buy Reserved Instances to cover the peak number of instances in the Auto Scaling group.`, ok: false, why: `It discounts instances that are only needed at peak, so most of the reservation sits idle.` },
      { t: `Switch the instances to a larger instance type so fewer are launched during spikes.`, ok: false, why: `Larger instances still serve static files from servers and cost more per hour.` },
      { t: `Store the static files on an Amazon EFS file system mounted by all instances, and turn on EFS Elastic Throughput for the spikes.`, ok: false, why: `The instances still serve every request, so the fleet still scales out.` },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "b4-020", mod: 13, domain: "secure", type: "mcq",
    q: `A pharmaceutical company keeps several petabytes of research data in a data lake governed by AWS Lake Formation across multiple AWS accounts. The data science team must share only selected tables and columns with the engineering team, which uses separate accounts. Permissions must be easy to maintain as more tables are added. Which solution meets these requirements with the <strong>LEAST</strong> operational overhead?`,
    options: [
      { t: `Use Lake Formation tag-based access control (LF-TBAC) to tag the shareable data and grant the engineering accounts cross-account permissions on those tags.`, ok: true, why: `New tables just get tagged and are shared automatically, without per-table grants.` },
      { t: `Copy the selected tables into a separate S3 bucket in each engineering account every night.`, ok: false, why: `Copying petabytes is costly, the copies go stale, and the pipelines need maintenance.` },
      { t: `Create IAM roles in the data science accounts that the engineering team assumes, and add S3 bucket policies on the data lake buckets that allow those roles to read only the selected prefixes.`, ok: false, why: `It bypasses Lake Formation and can't limit access to columns, and every new table needs new policy statements.` },
      { t: `Use Lake Formation named-resource grants to give each engineering account access to each table and column one by one.`, ok: false, why: `It works but every new table needs new grants, which adds overhead compared with tags.` },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "b4-021", mod: 9, domain: "performance", type: "mcq",
    q: `A design-collaboration startup lets users on several continents upload and download project files that are often several gigabytes each. Files are stored in a single Amazon S3 bucket in eu-central-1. Users far from Europe report slow transfers. The team wants a cost-effective way to cut upload and download latency without running servers. What should a solutions architect do to meet these requirements?`,
    options: [
      { t: `Enable S3 Transfer Acceleration on the bucket and have the application use the accelerate endpoint.`, ok: true, why: `Transfer Acceleration routes uploads and downloads through edge locations over the AWS backbone, with no servers to run.` },
      { t: `Create S3 buckets in five Regions and use S3 Cross-Region Replication to copy every file to all of them.`, ok: false, why: `Storing every file five times and paying replication costs is far more expensive.` },
      { t: `Run EC2 instances with large EBS volumes in each continent and sync files between them with scheduled jobs.`, ok: false, why: `It adds servers to manage and doesn't speed up the first upload.` },
      { t: `Put an AWS Global Accelerator accelerator in front of the S3 bucket endpoint.`, ok: false, why: `Global Accelerator endpoints are load balancers, EC2 instances, and Elastic IPs, not S3 buckets.` },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "b4-022", mod: 6, domain: "resilient", type: "multi",
    q: `An insurance broker's application uses one Amazon RDS DB instance and two hand-built EC2 web servers, all in a single Availability Zone. Last month an administrator deleted the DB instance by mistake and the application was down for a full day. The company wants to make the infrastructure as reliable as possible. Which combination of actions should a solutions architect take to meet these requirements? (Choose two.)`,
    options: [
      { t: `Convert the DB instance to a Multi-AZ deployment and turn on deletion protection.`, ok: true, why: `Multi-AZ survives an AZ failure, and deletion protection blocks an accidental delete.` },
      { t: `Put the web servers in an Auto Scaling group across multiple Availability Zones behind an Application Load Balancer.`, ok: true, why: `The ALB spreads traffic across AZs and the group replaces failed instances automatically.` },
      { t: `Add an RDS read replica in the same Availability Zone and keep the web servers as they are.`, ok: false, why: `A same-AZ replica doesn't survive an AZ outage, and the web tier stays a single point of failure.` },
      { t: `Take a manual snapshot of the DB instance every week and store it in another Region.`, ok: false, why: `Weekly snapshots mean days of data loss and a slow restore. They don't keep the app running.` },
      { t: `Move both web servers to a larger instance type in the same Availability Zone.`, ok: false, why: `Bigger instances in one AZ are still lost together if the AZ fails.` },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "b4-023", mod: 10, domain: "performance", type: "mcq",
    q: `A broadcaster stores 600 TB on an on-premises NAS and has a 10 Gbps AWS Direct Connect connection to AWS. A regulator gave the company 75 days to move the data to Amazon S3. Staff must keep reading and updating files on the NAS during the whole migration, and the transfer must not disrupt them. Which solution meets these requirements?`,
    options: [
      { t: `Deploy an AWS DataSync agent on premises and run DataSync tasks over Direct Connect to copy the NAS data to Amazon S3, with a final incremental run at cutover.`, ok: true, why: `At 10 Gbps, 600 TB moves in under two weeks, DataSync copies only changes on later runs, and users keep working on the NAS.` },
      { t: `Order AWS Snowball Edge devices, copy the NAS data onto them, and ship them to AWS.`, ok: false, why: `Shipping takes time and the copies miss every update made after loading, and the fast link is left unused.` },
      { t: `Mount the NAS on several on-premises Linux servers, run the AWS CLI s3 sync command on each of them in parallel over Direct Connect, and rerun the sync every night until the cutover date.`, ok: false, why: `It could work but needs scripting, retries, and tuning, and lacks DataSync's built-in verification and scheduling.` },
      { t: `Deploy an AWS Storage Gateway Volume Gateway in cached mode and point users at the new volumes.`, ok: false, why: `It changes how users access data and doesn't copy the existing NAS contents into S3 objects.` },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "b4-024", mod: 5, domain: "secure", type: "mcq",
    q: `A law firm stores signed contracts as PDF files in an existing Amazon S3 bucket. A new rule says every existing and future contract must be kept unchanged for 7 years, and no one, including administrators, may delete it early. Which solution meets these requirements with the <strong>LEAST</strong> operational overhead?`,
    options: [
      { t: `Turn on S3 Object Lock for the bucket with a default compliance-mode retention of 7 years, and use S3 Batch Operations to apply the same retention to existing objects.`, ok: true, why: `Compliance mode can't be shortened or bypassed, the default covers new uploads, and Batch Operations handles existing files in one job.` },
      { t: `Turn on S3 Object Lock with a default governance-mode retention of 7 years for new uploads.`, ok: false, why: `Users with the bypass permission can delete in governance mode, and existing objects aren't covered.` },
      { t: `Add a bucket policy that denies s3:DeleteObject to everyone, and turn on S3 Versioning.`, ok: false, why: `An administrator can edit or remove the bucket policy, so it doesn't prevent early deletion.` },
      { t: `Add an S3 Lifecycle rule that moves the contracts to S3 Glacier Deep Archive after 30 days, and restrict delete actions on the bucket with IAM policies attached to every role in the account.`, ok: false, why: `IAM policies can be changed by administrators, so nothing makes the files immutable.` },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "b4-025", mod: 9, domain: "resilient", type: "mcq",
    q: `A company runs a stateless REST API built with Amazon API Gateway and AWS Lambda in us-east-1 and eu-west-1. Users should reach the closest Region, and if one Region fails, all traffic must go to the other automatically. The company already hosts its domain in Amazon Route 53. Which solution meets these requirements?`,
    options: [
      { t: `Create latency-based Route 53 records for the two Regional API endpoints, each tied to a health check.`, ok: true, why: `Latency routing sends users to the nearest Region, and a failed health check removes that Region's record.` },
      { t: `Create a weighted Route 53 record that sends 50% of traffic to each Region, without health checks.`, ok: false, why: `Without health checks, half of users keep going to a failed Region, and weighting ignores proximity.` },
      { t: `Create an AWS Global Accelerator accelerator with an endpoint group in each Region that points to that Region's API Gateway API.`, ok: false, why: `Global Accelerator endpoints are ALBs, NLBs, EC2 instances, and Elastic IPs, not API Gateway APIs.` },
      { t: `Create a simple Route 53 record that lists both Regional API endpoints as values.`, ok: false, why: `Simple routing returns values at random and can't use health checks, so failed endpoints still get traffic.` },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "b4-026", mod: 10, domain: "resilient", type: "mcq",
    q: `A company has two VPCs, Ops and Prod. Ops connects to the data center through two Site-to-Site VPN tunnels that both end on one customer gateway device. Prod uses a virtual private gateway with two AWS Direct Connect connections at different locations. The VPCs talk to each other over one VPC peering connection. The company wants to remove every single point of failure in this design. Which solution meets these requirements?`,
    options: [
      { t: `Add a second customer gateway device in the data center and create a second set of VPN tunnels from Ops to it.`, ok: true, why: `The one on-premises device is the single point of failure. A second device with its own tunnels removes it.` },
      { t: `Add a second VPC peering connection between Ops and Prod.`, ok: false, why: `A peering connection isn't a single device that can fail, and AWS allows only one between the same two VPCs.` },
      { t: `Add a third Direct Connect connection to the Prod VPC's virtual private gateway.`, ok: false, why: `Prod already has two redundant connections. The weak spot is the Ops VPN.` },
      { t: `Attach a second virtual private gateway to the Ops VPC and move one of the two existing VPN tunnels to it so each tunnel ends on a different gateway.`, ok: false, why: `A VPC can have only one virtual private gateway, and the AWS side is already redundant.` },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "b4-027", mod: 10, domain: "cost", type: "mcq",
    q: `An insurer runs a claims application on an on-premises Oracle database. The team has little capacity for database administration, backups, or hardware upkeep and wants to move to AWS quickly. A third-party monitoring agent must be installed on the database host and needs operating system-level privileges. Which solution migrates the database <strong>MOST</strong> cost-effectively?`,
    options: [
      { t: `Migrate to Amazon RDS Custom for Oracle and install the third-party agent on the host.`, ok: true, why: `RDS Custom gives OS access for the agent while AWS still handles backups and much of the administration.` },
      { t: `Migrate to Amazon RDS for Oracle and request OS access from AWS Support for the agent.`, ok: false, why: `Standard RDS doesn't allow OS-level access, so the agent can't be installed.` },
      { t: `Migrate to Oracle on EC2 instances and manage backups, patching, and high availability in-house.`, ok: false, why: `It allows the agent but leaves all the admin work the team wants to get rid of.` },
      { t: `Convert the schema with AWS SCT and migrate to Amazon Aurora PostgreSQL-Compatible with AWS DMS.`, ok: false, why: `Converting Oracle code takes time and effort, which isn't a quick move, and the agent still needs OS access.` },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "b4-028", mod: 6, domain: "resilient", type: "multi",
    q: `A small company runs its web, application, and database tiers on one on-premises server. It wants to move to AWS and follow the AWS Well-Architected Framework, with best practices for security, scalability, and resilience. The company expects traffic to grow quickly after launch and can't accept downtime from a single failed server. Which combination of solutions meets these requirements? (Choose three.)`,
    options: [
      { t: `Create a VPC across two Availability Zones with each tier in its own private subnets, and run the web and application tiers in Auto Scaling groups.`, ok: true, why: `Separate private subnets per tier and multi-AZ Auto Scaling give isolation and scaling.` },
      { t: `Put an Elastic Load Balancer in public subnets in front of the web tier.`, ok: true, why: `The load balancer is the only public entry point and spreads traffic across AZs.` },
      { t: `Run the database on Amazon RDS with a Multi-AZ deployment in private subnets.`, ok: true, why: `RDS Multi-AZ gives managed failover and keeps the database off the internet.` },
      { t: `Lift and shift the whole server to one large EC2 instance in a public subnet with an Elastic IP.`, ok: false, why: `One public instance is a single point of failure and exposes all tiers to the internet.` },
      { t: `Put all three tiers in public subnets and lock them down with security groups only.`, ok: false, why: `Databases and app servers don't need public subnets. Private subnets are the defense in depth the framework expects.` },
      { t: `Run the database on an EC2 instance in one Availability Zone and back it up nightly to Amazon S3.`, ok: false, why: `A single-AZ database fails with its AZ, and nightly backups mean up to a day of data loss.` },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "b4-029", mod: 10, domain: "secure", type: "multi",
    q: `A manufacturer is moving to AWS. It will run containers on Amazon ECS with the EC2 launch type, connect its data center through AWS Direct Connect, and use Amazon RDS for its databases. The operations manager wants to know which tasks stay with the company's own team under the shared responsibility model. Which activities must the company's own team handle to meet these requirements? (Choose three.)`,
    options: [
      { t: `Patching the operating system and installing monitoring and intrusion detection agents on the ECS container instances.`, ok: true, why: `With the EC2 launch type, the customer owns the guest OS of the container instances.` },
      { t: `Creating database users and granting them permissions in the RDS databases.`, ok: true, why: `AWS runs the engine, but users, schemas, and data access stay with the customer.` },
      { t: `Encrypting data that travels over the Direct Connect link, for example with a VPN or MACsec.`, ok: true, why: `Direct Connect isn't encrypted by default, so protecting data in transit is the customer's job.` },
      { t: `Patching the database engine's underlying host operating system for RDS.`, ok: false, why: `AWS manages the host OS for RDS.` },
      { t: `Maintaining the physical Direct Connect equipment inside the AWS Direct Connect location's AWS cage.`, ok: false, why: `AWS maintains its own equipment at the location.` },
      { t: `Replacing failed disks in the storage that backs the RDS DB instances.`, ok: false, why: `Physical hardware is AWS's responsibility.` },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "b4-030", mod: 8, domain: "cost", type: "mcq",
    q: `A company runs a Python cleanup script on a dedicated EC2 instance. A cron job starts it every 30 minutes, it needs about 1 GB of memory, and it finishes in under 20 seconds. The instance is idle almost all the time apart from short CPU spikes during each run. The company wants to cut the cost of running this job. Which solution meets these requirements?`,
    options: [
      { t: `Move the script to an AWS Lambda function with 1 GB of memory and run it from an Amazon EventBridge Scheduler schedule every 30 minutes.`, ok: true, why: `Lambda bills only for the seconds the script runs, so an always-on instance is no longer needed.` },
      { t: `Move the script to a smaller burstable T-family instance and keep the cron job.`, ok: false, why: `It still pays for an instance 24/7 for a job that runs a few minutes a day.` },
      { t: `Buy a 1-year Reserved Instance for the current instance.`, ok: false, why: `It discounts an instance that is idle almost all the time, which is still wasteful.` },
      { t: `Run the script as an AWS Batch job on a managed compute environment of On-Demand Instances, triggered by an Amazon EventBridge schedule every 30 minutes.`, ok: false, why: `Batch spins up instances for each run, which adds startup time and cost for a 20-second job.` },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "b4-031", mod: 14, domain: "secure", type: "mcq",
    q: `A bank must back up Amazon EC2 instances and several Amazon S3 buckets. Regulations say each backup must be kept for a set period and must not be altered or deleted by anyone during that period, including the root user. The bank wants a managed service rather than custom scripts, and auditors will check that no one can shorten the retention. Which solution meets these requirements?`,
    options: [
      { t: `Create an AWS Backup vault with AWS Backup Vault Lock in compliance mode, and create a backup plan that stores the backups in it.`, ok: true, why: `Compliance-mode Vault Lock makes backups immutable for the retention period, even for root.` },
      { t: `Create an AWS Backup vault with AWS Backup Vault Lock in governance mode with minimum and maximum retention periods, and create a backup plan that stores the backups in it.`, ok: false, why: `Users with the right IAM permissions can remove a governance-mode lock, so backups aren't fully immutable.` },
      { t: `Create EBS snapshots and S3 copies with a Lambda function and protect them with IAM policies that deny deletion.`, ok: false, why: `Admins can change IAM policies, and the root user isn't bound by them.` },
      { t: `Create a backup plan in AWS Backup and add an SCP that denies deleting recovery points.`, ok: false, why: `SCPs don't apply to the management account and can be edited by admins, so they aren't a regulator-grade lock.` },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "b4-032", mod: 12, domain: "performance", type: "mcq",
    q: `A newly hired architect at a retailer finds that resources are spread across many AWS accounts and Regions with no documentation. The architect needs to see how workloads fit together, including relationships between resources, and produce architecture diagrams for each account. Which solution meets these requirements in the <strong>MOST</strong> operationally efficient way?`,
    options: [
      { t: `Deploy Workload Discovery on AWS to collect resources across accounts and Regions and generate architecture diagrams.`, ok: true, why: `It discovers resources and their relationships across accounts and draws diagrams automatically.` },
      { t: `Use AWS Systems Manager Inventory to list installed software on each managed EC2 instance.`, ok: false, why: `Inventory covers software on managed instances, not relationships between AWS resources.` },
      { t: `Export each account's resources with the AWS CLI into spreadsheets and draw the diagrams by hand.`, ok: false, why: `It's slow manual work and goes out of date quickly.` },
      { t: `Use AWS Trusted Advisor in each account to review the resources and their configuration.`, ok: false, why: `Trusted Advisor gives best-practice checks, not resource maps or diagrams.` },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "b4-033", mod: 14, domain: "cost", type: "multi",
    q: `A company uses AWS Organizations and gives each project account its own monthly budget. When an account reaches its budget, the finance team must be alerted, and the account must automatically stop launching new resources until the next period. Finance wants this enforced automatically, without anyone watching dashboards. Which combination of solutions meets these requirements? (Choose three.)`,
    options: [
      { t: `Create a budget in AWS Budgets for each project account with the allocated amount.`, ok: true, why: `Budgets tracks spend against a set amount per account.` },
      { t: `Create an IAM role that AWS Budgets can assume to run budget actions.`, ok: true, why: `Budget actions need a role with permission to apply policies or SCPs.` },
      { t: `Add an alert on the budget threshold that notifies finance and runs a budget action that applies an SCP blocking new resource creation.`, ok: true, why: `The alert notifies finance, and the action automatically applies the restriction when the threshold is hit.` },
      { t: `Use AWS Cost Explorer to forecast spend each week and email finance a report.`, ok: false, why: `Reports don't stop new resources automatically when the budget is hit.` },
      { t: `Create an AWS Config rule that flags any new resource when spending is high.`, ok: false, why: `Config can't read budget spend and doesn't block provisioning.` },
      { t: `Enable AWS Cost Anomaly Detection on each account to stop resource creation when costs spike.`, ok: false, why: `It flags unusual spend but can't block provisioning or enforce a set budget.` },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "b4-034", mod: 14, domain: "cost", type: "mcq",
    q: `A company runs its applications on Amazon EC2 instances in eu-west-1. For disaster recovery it needs copies of the instances in eu-north-1, the ability to launch them there, and central management from one AWS account. It wants to avoid building custom scripts, and an RPO of 24 hours is acceptable. Which solution meets these requirements <strong>MOST</strong> cost-effectively?`,
    options: [
      { t: `Create an AWS Backup plan for the EC2 instances with a cross-Region copy rule to eu-north-1.`, ok: true, why: `AWS Backup handles scheduled backups and cross-Region copies centrally, with no extra running servers.` },
      { t: `Use AWS Elastic Disaster Recovery to continuously replicate the instances to a staging area in eu-north-1.`, ok: false, why: `It gives lower RPO but costs more due to continuous replication and staging resources, which the scenario doesn't need.` },
      { t: `Write a Lambda function that creates AMIs every night and copies them to eu-north-1.`, ok: false, why: `It works but is the custom scripting the company wants to avoid.` },
      { t: `Run a second copy of every instance in eu-north-1 at all times and keep them in sync.`, ok: false, why: `Running a full duplicate fleet costs far more than storing backups.` },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "b4-035", mod: 8, domain: "performance", type: "multi",
    q: `A fintech team is building a REST API in Amazon API Gateway for a cashback calculator. Each request needs about 2 GB of memory and up to 3 GB of temporary disk space, and finishes in a few seconds. The results must be stored in a relational database. The team wants the least administration possible. Which additional AWS services meet these requirements? (Choose two.)`,
    options: [
      { t: `AWS Lambda`, ok: true, why: `Lambda supports up to 10 GB of memory and 10 GB of ephemeral storage, with no servers to manage.` },
      { t: `Amazon RDS`, ok: true, why: `RDS is a managed relational database, so backups and patching are handled for you.` },
      { t: `Amazon EC2`, ok: false, why: `It works but adds instances to patch and scale.` },
      { t: `Amazon DynamoDB`, ok: false, why: `DynamoDB is a key-value store, not relational.` },
      { t: `Amazon ElastiCache`, ok: false, why: `ElastiCache is an in-memory cache, not a relational system of record.` },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "b4-036", mod: 14, domain: "cost", type: "mcq",
    q: `A company uses AWS Organizations with dozens of accounts. A tag policy makes sure every resource gets a team tag. The accounting group must see Amazon EC2 spending per team across all accounts in AWS Cost Explorer from one place. Which solution meets these requirements in the <strong>MOST</strong> operationally efficient way?`,
    options: [
      { t: `In the management account's billing console, activate team as a user-defined cost allocation tag, then build one Cost Explorer report filtered to EC2 and grouped by the team tag.`, ok: true, why: `Activating the tag once in the management account applies to all member accounts, and one report shows spend per team.` },
      { t: `Activate the team cost allocation tag in each member account and build a Cost Explorer report in each one.`, ok: false, why: `Cost allocation tags are activated in the management account, and per-account reports don't give one view.` },
      { t: `Activate the AWS-generated createdBy cost allocation tag and group the Cost Explorer report by it.`, ok: false, why: `createdBy shows who created a resource, not which team owns it.` },
      { t: `Export the Cost and Usage Report from each account to Amazon S3 and query it with Athena by team.`, ok: false, why: `It works but needs a pipeline and SQL queries, more effort than a Cost Explorer report.` },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "b4-037", mod: 13, domain: "secure", type: "mcq",
    q: `A sales team keeps customer data in Salesforce and wants to sync it into Amazon S3 every hour for analytics. The data must be encrypted in transit and encrypted at rest with an AWS KMS customer managed key. API access is already enabled on the Salesforce account. The team doesn't want to write or host integration code. Which solution meets these requirements?`,
    options: [
      { t: `Create an Amazon AppFlow flow from Salesforce to Amazon S3 that runs every hour and uses the customer managed KMS key.`, ok: true, why: `AppFlow is a managed SaaS connector that encrypts in transit and lets you pick a customer managed key, with no code.` },
      { t: `Write AWS Lambda functions that call the Salesforce API every hour on an EventBridge schedule and write the results to S3 with SSE-KMS.`, ok: false, why: `It works but is custom integration code the team wanted to avoid.` },
      { t: `Use AWS DataSync with a Salesforce location to copy data to S3.`, ok: false, why: `DataSync moves file and object storage data. It has no Salesforce connector.` },
      { t: `Use AWS Storage Gateway S3 File Gateway and have Salesforce export files to the gateway share.`, ok: false, why: `Salesforce can't write to a file share, and it adds a gateway appliance to run.` },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "b4-038", mod: 9, domain: "performance", type: "mcq",
    q: `A studio is launching a multiplayer mobile game hosted on EC2 instances in an Auto Scaling group in us-east-2, with player data in Amazon DynamoDB. The game sends both TCP and UDP traffic between players and servers. Players will be worldwide, and the company wants the lowest possible latency for all of them. Which solution meets these requirements?`,
    options: [
      { t: `Register the instances with a Network Load Balancer that listens on the TCP and UDP ports, and put an AWS Global Accelerator accelerator in front of it.`, ok: true, why: `Global Accelerator brings players onto the AWS backbone at the nearest edge and supports TCP and UDP, and the NLB handles both protocols.` },
      { t: `Register the instances with an Application Load Balancer and put an Amazon CloudFront distribution in front of it.`, ok: false, why: `ALB and CloudFront handle HTTP/HTTPS only, so UDP game traffic can't pass.` },
      { t: `Register the instances with a Network Load Balancer and put an Amazon CloudFront distribution in front of it.`, ok: false, why: `CloudFront doesn't proxy raw UDP traffic, so the game packets can't use it.` },
      { t: `Use Amazon Route 53 latency-based routing directly to the instances' public IP addresses.`, ok: false, why: `There is only one Region, so latency routing has no closer choice, and traffic still travels the public internet.` },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "b4-039", mod: 7, domain: "resilient", type: "mcq",
    q: `A ticket reseller's web application writes orders straight from an EC2 instance into an Amazon Aurora MySQL database. During flash sales the application can't keep up and some orders fail. The company needs every order to be written to the database reliably and as quickly as the database allows, even during spikes. What should a solutions architect do to meet these requirements?`,
    options: [
      { t: `Send orders to an Amazon SQS queue and have EC2 instances in an Auto Scaling group read from the queue and write to Aurora.`, ok: true, why: `The queue stores every order durably during spikes, and the consumer fleet scales to write them as fast as Aurora can take.` },
      { t: `Send orders to an Amazon SNS topic and subscribe the application's EC2 instance to the topic with an HTTPS endpoint that writes each order to Aurora.`, ok: false, why: `SNS pushes messages without storage, so if the instance can't keep up, orders can still be lost.` },
      { t: `Add an Aurora Replica and point the application's writes at the reader endpoint.`, ok: false, why: `Replicas are read-only. Writes must go to the writer instance.` },
      { t: `Increase the size of the EC2 instance so it can process more orders at once.`, ok: false, why: `A bigger single instance still has a ceiling and is a single point of failure.` },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "b4-040", mod: 13, domain: "cost", type: "mcq",
    q: `A smart-thermostat maker collects about 3 MB of data per device each night into an Amazon S3 bucket. Each file must be summarized, and results must be available as soon as possible after upload. Processing one file takes 1 GB of memory and about 40 seconds. Upload volume varies a lot from night to night. Which solution meets these requirements <strong>MOST</strong> cost-effectively?`,
    options: [
      { t: `Invoke an AWS Lambda function from S3 event notifications to process each file as it arrives.`, ok: true, why: `Lambda runs only when files arrive and scales with the volume, so there is no idle cost.` },
      { t: `Run an AWS Glue Spark job every night to process all files uploaded that day.`, ok: false, why: `A nightly batch delays results, and Spark is overkill for small files.` },
      { t: `Run an Amazon EMR cluster around the clock with a Spark Streaming job that picks up new files as they arrive.`, ok: false, why: `An always-on cluster costs far more for small files that take seconds each.` },
      { t: `Run an EC2 instance that polls the bucket every minute and processes new files.`, ok: false, why: `It pays for an idle instance most of the day and can't scale with busy nights.` },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "b4-041", mod: 6, domain: "resilient", type: "mcq",
    q: `A pet-supply retailer stores all orders in an Amazon RDS for MySQL Single-AZ DB instance. Leadership wants to remove single points of failure and cut database downtime during failures and maintenance. The development team has no time this quarter, so the solution must not require any application code changes. Which solution meets these requirements?`,
    options: [
      { t: `Modify the DB instance to a Multi-AZ deployment.`, ok: true, why: `RDS keeps a synchronous standby in another AZ and fails over behind the same endpoint, so the code doesn't change.` },
      { t: `Create a read replica in another Availability Zone and update the application to promote and switch to it during an outage.`, ok: false, why: `Promotion is manual and the app must change its endpoint, which needs code and runbook changes.` },
      { t: `Take automated snapshots every hour and restore the latest snapshot to a new DB instance when the primary fails.`, ok: false, why: `Restoring takes a long time and loses up to an hour of orders.` },
      { t: `Migrate the database to an EC2 instance in an Auto Scaling group with a minimum and maximum size of one.`, ok: false, why: `A replacement instance starts with no data, and the team takes on database admin work.` },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "b4-042", mod: 5, domain: "resilient", type: "mcq",
    q: `A company is building a clustered application that runs on several Nitro-based EC2 instances in the same Availability Zone. The clustering software coordinates writes, and every node must be able to write to the same block storage volume at the same time so another node can take over instantly if one fails. Which solution meets these requirements?`,
    options: [
      { t: `Use a Provisioned IOPS SSD (io2) EBS volume with Multi-Attach enabled and attach it to all the instances.`, ok: true, why: `io1 and io2 volumes support Multi-Attach to up to 16 Nitro instances in the same AZ.` },
      { t: `Use a General Purpose SSD (gp3) EBS volume and attach it to all the instances.`, ok: false, why: `gp3 volumes don't support Multi-Attach.` },
      { t: `Use an Amazon EFS file system mounted on all the instances.`, ok: false, why: `EFS is shared file storage, not a block volume the clustering software can write to directly.` },
      { t: `Use instance store volumes and replicate the data between nodes with the application.`, ok: false, why: `Instance store is local to one instance and lost when it stops, so nodes don't share a volume.` },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "b4-043", mod: 14, domain: "cost", type: "mcq",
    q: `A company uses AWS Organizations with consolidated billing. One member account bought a 3-year Compute Savings Plan, but after a workload change that account now uses less than half of its commitment. Other accounts in the organization run plenty of EC2 and Fargate workloads at On-Demand rates. Which solution meets these requirements with the <strong>LEAST</strong> effort?`,
    options: [
      { t: `Make sure Savings Plans discount sharing is turned on in the billing preferences of the management account, including for the member account that owns the plan.`, ok: true, why: `With sharing on, unused commitment applies to eligible usage in other accounts of the organization.` },
      { t: `Sell the unused part of the Savings Plan on the Reserved Instance Marketplace.`, ok: false, why: `Savings Plans can't be sold on the marketplace.` },
      { t: `Migrate the other accounts' workloads into the member account that owns the plan.`, ok: false, why: `Moving workloads between accounts is a lot of work just to use a discount.` },
      { t: `Contact AWS Support to transfer the Savings Plan to the management account.`, ok: false, why: `Savings Plans can't be moved between accounts. Discount sharing is the supported way.` },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "b4-044", mod: 8, domain: "secure", type: "mcq",
    q: `An online bookstore is building a search catalog as microservices. Customers must reach the service through a public REST API. The backend containers run on Amazon ECS in private subnets and must not be exposed to the internet directly. Traffic varies widely during sales events, and the team wants managed AWS services for the API layer. Which solution meets these requirements?`,
    options: [
      { t: `Build the REST API in Amazon API Gateway, put a Network Load Balancer in front of the ECS service in the private subnets, and connect them with an API Gateway VPC link.`, ok: true, why: `A VPC link lets API Gateway reach private targets through an NLB without making the containers public.` },
      { t: `Build the REST API in Amazon API Gateway and point the integration at the private IP addresses of the ECS tasks.`, ok: false, why: `API Gateway can't reach private IPs inside a VPC without a VPC link.` },
      { t: `Run the ECS tasks in public subnets with public IP addresses and point API Gateway at them.`, ok: false, why: `It exposes the containers directly to the internet.` },
      { t: `Put the ECS service behind an internal Application Load Balancer and give customers its DNS name.`, ok: false, why: `An internal load balancer can't be reached from the internet, so customers can't call it.` },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "b4-045", mod: 5, domain: "cost", type: "mcq",
    q: `An analytics company keeps raw customer data in Amazon S3. Each client asks for different kinds of analysis, so some objects are read daily and others not for months, and the company can't predict which. It wants to lower its S3 storage bill without slowing down reads or adding retrieval fees when data is accessed. Which solution meets these requirements?`,
    options: [
      { t: `Use an S3 Lifecycle rule to move objects from S3 Standard to S3 Intelligent-Tiering.`, ok: true, why: `Intelligent-Tiering moves each object between tiers based on its own access, with no retrieval fees.` },
      { t: `Use an S3 Lifecycle rule to move objects to S3 Standard-IA after 30 days.`, ok: false, why: `Objects that stay busy pay retrieval fees in Standard-IA, which can raise costs.` },
      { t: `Use an S3 Lifecycle rule to move objects to S3 Glacier Instant Retrieval after 30 days.`, ok: false, why: `Frequently read objects would pay high retrieval fees.` },
      { t: `Use S3 Storage Lens to review access patterns every month and move objects by hand.`, ok: false, why: `Manual moves can't keep up with access patterns that the company can't predict.` },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "b4-046", mod: 3, domain: "secure", type: "mcq",
    q: `A company runs EC2 instances that use only IPv6 addresses in a public subnet. The instances must call external APIs on the internet. The security policy says no host on the internet may open a connection to the instances. The team wants a managed AWS component rather than a fleet of proxy servers. Which solution meets these requirements?`,
    options: [
      { t: `Create an egress-only internet gateway and add a ::/0 route to it in the subnet's route table.`, ok: true, why: `An egress-only internet gateway allows IPv6 traffic out and blocks connections started from the internet.` },
      { t: `Create a NAT gateway and add a ::/0 route to it in the subnet's route table.`, ok: false, why: `A NAT gateway translates IPv4 traffic. It doesn't provide stateful egress-only IPv6 access by itself.` },
      { t: `Keep the internet gateway route and add a network ACL inbound deny rule for ::/0.`, ok: false, why: `NACLs are stateless, so denying all inbound IPv6 also drops the replies to outbound calls.` },
      { t: `Create a virtual private gateway and route ::/0 traffic through it.`, ok: false, why: `A virtual private gateway is for VPN and Direct Connect, not internet access.` },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "b4-047", mod: 3, domain: "cost", type: "mcq",
    q: `A company runs containers in private subnets that read and write about 2 TB of data a day to an Amazon S3 bucket in the same Region. Today the traffic goes through a NAT gateway, and data processing charges are high. The company wants to cut costs and keep the traffic off the internet where possible. Which solution meets these requirements?`,
    options: [
      { t: `Create a gateway VPC endpoint for Amazon S3 and associate it with the private subnets' route tables.`, ok: true, why: `Gateway endpoints for S3 have no hourly or data charges and keep traffic on the AWS network.` },
      { t: `Create an interface VPC endpoint for Amazon S3 in each private subnet.`, ok: false, why: `It keeps traffic private but charges per hour and per GB, so it costs more than a gateway endpoint.` },
      { t: `Move the containers to public subnets and give them public IP addresses.`, ok: false, why: `Traffic then goes over the internet path, and the containers become reachable from outside.` },
      { t: `Add a second NAT gateway in another Availability Zone and split the private subnets' S3 traffic between the two gateways.`, ok: false, why: `It adds another hourly charge and still pays NAT processing per GB.` },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "b4-048", mod: 6, domain: "performance", type: "mcq",
    q: `A messaging app stores chat messages in an Amazon DynamoDB table. Users open the same recent conversations many times, and the team wants reads of those messages to come back in microseconds. The team wants the fewest possible application changes and no separate cache logic to maintain. Which solution meets these requirements?`,
    options: [
      { t: `Add a DynamoDB Accelerator (DAX) cluster for the table and point the app's DynamoDB client at the DAX endpoint.`, ok: true, why: `DAX is a write-through cache that uses the DynamoDB API, so only the client endpoint changes.` },
      { t: `Add an Amazon ElastiCache for Redis OSS cluster in front of the table and write cache-aside logic for every read and write.`, ok: false, why: `It works but needs new caching code and invalidation logic.` },
      { t: `Add DynamoDB read replicas in the same Region and spread reads across them.`, ok: false, why: `DynamoDB has no read replicas, and more throughput wouldn't give microsecond reads.` },
      { t: `Switch the table to on-demand capacity mode.`, ok: false, why: `On-demand changes billing and scaling, not read latency.` },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "b4-049", mod: 10, domain: "performance", type: "mcq",
    q: `A company has dozens of VPCs in five AWS Regions. Until now each Region's workloads were isolated, but a new application requires every VPC to reach every other VPC in all Regions. The networking team is small and wants to avoid managing hundreds of connections. Which solution meets these requirements with the <strong>LEAST</strong> administrative effort?`,
    options: [
      { t: `Create a transit gateway in each Region, attach that Region's VPCs, and connect the transit gateways with inter-Region peering.`, ok: true, why: `Each VPC needs one attachment, and a few peerings connect the Regions, instead of a full mesh.` },
      { t: `Create VPC peering connections between every pair of VPCs and update every route table.`, ok: false, why: `A full mesh across dozens of VPCs means hundreds of peerings and routes to maintain.` },
      { t: `Create Site-to-Site VPN connections between virtual private gateways in each VPC.`, ok: false, why: `VPN between VPCs is slow to set up, limited in bandwidth, and still a mesh to manage.` },
      { t: `Use AWS PrivateLink to expose each VPC to every other VPC.`, ok: false, why: `PrivateLink exposes specific services, not full network routing between VPCs.` },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "b4-050", mod: 14, domain: "resilient", type: "mcq",
    q: `A company is building a containerized Linux application on Amazon ECS that needs a shared, highly durable file system with mount targets in every Availability Zone of the Region. The data must be recoverable in another Region with an RPO of 6 hours, and the team wants to manage that cross-Region copy with AWS Backup. Which solution meets these requirements?`,
    options: [
      { t: `Use Amazon EFS with the Standard storage class and an AWS Backup plan that copies backups to another Region every few hours.`, ok: true, why: `EFS Standard has mount targets in every AZ, ECS mounts it natively, and AWS Backup does scheduled cross-Region copies.` },
      { t: `Use Amazon EFS One Zone with an AWS Backup plan that copies backups to another Region.`, ok: false, why: `One Zone has a single mount target AZ, so it doesn't give mount targets in every AZ.` },
      { t: `Use Amazon FSx for Lustre with a scratch deployment type and export changed data to an Amazon S3 bucket in another Region every few hours.`, ok: false, why: `Scratch file systems aren't durable, and it doesn't use AWS Backup for the copy.` },
      { t: `Use an io2 EBS volume with Multi-Attach and copy snapshots to another Region with AWS Backup.`, ok: false, why: `EBS volumes live in one AZ, so they can't be mounted from every AZ.` },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "b4-051", mod: 12, domain: "secure", type: "mcq",
    q: `A fast-growing agency is onboarding many new employees. The architect has created IAM groups by department and will add each new user to the right group. The architect now needs to give these users the permissions they need for their jobs. Which additional action is the <strong>MOST</strong> secure way to grant permissions to the new users?`,
    options: [
      { t: `Create IAM policies that grant only the permissions each department needs and attach them to the matching groups.`, ok: true, why: `Group policies scale as users join and least privilege limits damage from mistakes.` },
      { t: `Attach the AdministratorAccess managed policy to each group, and use AWS CloudTrail to review what each user does every week.`, ok: false, why: `It gives everyone full access. Logging doesn't prevent misuse.` },
      { t: `Attach least-privilege policies directly to each user as they join.`, ok: false, why: `It can be secure, but per-user policies drift and are harder to manage than group policies.` },
      { t: `Create one IAM role with broad permissions and let every group assume it.`, ok: false, why: `One broad role gives every department more access than it needs.` },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "b4-052", mod: 5, domain: "secure", type: "mcq",
    q: `A city council must publish several hundred public notices as files that anyone on the internet can read. Until a date set in law, nobody, including the council's own administrators, may modify or delete these files. The council wants a simple, low-cost setup that it configures once. Which solution meets these requirements in the <strong>MOST</strong> secure way?`,
    options: [
      { t: `Create an S3 bucket with Versioning and Object Lock, set compliance-mode retention until the legal date, enable static website hosting, and add a bucket policy that allows public read only.`, ok: true, why: `Compliance mode blocks changes and deletes by anyone until the date, and the policy allows only reads.` },
      { t: `Create an S3 bucket with static website hosting and a bucket policy that allows public read and denies s3:DeleteObject to everyone.`, ok: false, why: `An administrator can change the bucket policy and then delete or overwrite files.` },
      { t: `Host the files on an EC2 web server with a read-only EBS volume, and restrict SSH access to one administrator.`, ok: false, why: `The administrator can still change the files, and the server needs patching.` },
      { t: `Create an S3 bucket with Versioning and Object Lock in governance mode, and allow public read with a bucket policy.`, ok: false, why: `Users with bypass permission can delete in governance mode, so admins aren't fully blocked.` },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "b4-053", mod: 12, domain: "resilient", type: "mcq",
    q: `A company built a prototype environment by hand: an Auto Scaling group, an Application Load Balancer, and an Amazon RDS database. After testing, it wants to deploy identical copies for development and production across two Availability Zones, quickly and in an automated, repeatable way. What should a solutions architect recommend to meet these requirements?`,
    options: [
      { t: `Write an AWS CloudFormation template based on the prototype and deploy it as a stack for each environment.`, ok: true, why: `Infrastructure as code creates the same resources every time, with parameters for each environment.` },
      { t: `Document every console step from the prototype and have engineers repeat them for each environment.`, ok: false, why: `Manual steps are slow and error-prone, and they aren't automated.` },
      { t: `Create an AMI from a prototype instance and launch it in each environment.`, ok: false, why: `An AMI only covers the instance, not the load balancer, Auto Scaling group, or database.` },
      { t: `Use AWS Systems Manager Run Command to run the same setup scripts that were used for the prototype in each new environment.`, ok: false, why: `Run Command runs commands on existing instances. It doesn't create the full environment.` },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "b4-054", mod: 6, domain: "performance", type: "mcq",
    q: `A furniture store runs a three-tier web app with an Amazon RDS for MySQL Multi-AZ database and an Amazon ElastiCache cluster as its cache. When a customer adds an item, the item must be written to the cache at the same time, and the cache must never return data that differs from the database. Which caching strategy meets these requirements?`,
    options: [
      { t: `Write-through: update the cache every time the application writes to the database.`, ok: true, why: `Every write goes to both, so the cache always holds current data.` },
      { t: `Lazy loading: load an item into the cache only when a read request finds that it's missing.`, ok: false, why: `New items don't enter the cache until someone reads them, and cached items can go stale.` },
      { t: `Lazy loading with a short time to live on every key.`, ok: false, why: `A TTL limits staleness but data can still differ from the database until it expires.` },
      { t: `Warm the cache once a night from a full database export.`, ok: false, why: `Items added during the day aren't in the cache, and changes show up a day late.` },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "b4-055", mod: 8, domain: "cost", type: "mcq",
    q: `A company has containerized a .NET 6 job that runs in a Windows container. The job must run every 15 minutes and takes between 1 and 4 minutes each time. The job reads its input from Amazon S3 and has no other dependencies. The company wants to stop managing servers for it. Which solution meets these requirements <strong>MOST</strong> cost-effectively?`,
    options: [
      { t: `Run the job as an Amazon ECS scheduled task on AWS Fargate, using an EventBridge schedule every 15 minutes.`, ok: true, why: `Fargate runs Windows containers and bills only while each task runs, with no servers to manage.` },
      { t: `Run the job in AWS Lambda from a container image, scheduled every 15 minutes with EventBridge.`, ok: false, why: `Lambda container images must be Linux-based, so a Windows container can't run there.` },
      { t: `Run the job on an Amazon ECS cluster backed by Windows EC2 instances that run all the time.`, ok: false, why: `It pays for Windows instances 24/7 for a few minutes of work each hour.` },
      { t: `Run the job on an Amazon EKS cluster with Windows managed node groups and a Kubernetes CronJob every 15 minutes.`, ok: false, why: `It adds the EKS control plane fee plus always-on Windows nodes, which costs more.` },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "b4-056", mod: 11, domain: "secure", type: "multi",
    q: `A holding company runs dozens of standalone AWS accounts and plans to create many more for new business units. It wants one multi-account structure, and employees must sign in to all accounts with their existing corporate directory credentials. Which combination of actions should a solutions architect recommend to meet these requirements? (Choose two.)`,
    options: [
      { t: `Create an organization in AWS Organizations with all features enabled, invite the existing accounts, and create the new accounts in it.`, ok: true, why: `Organizations centralizes the accounts and is required for IAM Identity Center across them.` },
      { t: `Enable AWS IAM Identity Center in the organization and connect it to the corporate directory as the identity source.`, ok: true, why: `Identity Center gives single sign-on to every account using directory credentials.` },
      { t: `Create IAM users in every account with the same user names as in the corporate directory.`, ok: false, why: `Separate IAM users per account means separate passwords and lots of manual work.` },
      { t: `Create an Amazon Cognito user pool and federate it with the corporate directory for console sign-in.`, ok: false, why: `Cognito is for application users, not workforce access to AWS accounts.` },
      { t: `Create an organization in AWS Organizations with only consolidated billing features enabled.`, ok: false, why: `Billing-only organizations don't support IAM Identity Center or SCPs.` },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "b4-057", mod: 5, domain: "cost", type: "mcq",
    q: `A news archive wants to store decades of old video footage in AWS at the lowest possible price. The files are rarely needed, but when an editor asks for one, it must be available within about 5 minutes. The archive is several petabytes and grows every month. Which solution is the <strong>MOST</strong> cost-effective?`,
    options: [
      { t: `Store the footage in S3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval and use Expedited retrievals when an editor needs a file.`, ok: true, why: `Flexible Retrieval has very low storage cost, and Expedited retrieval usually returns data in 1 to 5 minutes.` },
      { t: `Store the footage in S3 Glacier Deep Archive and request Standard retrievals whenever an editor needs a specific file.`, ok: false, why: `Deep Archive retrievals take up to 12 hours, which misses the 5-minute need.` },
      { t: `Store the footage in S3 Standard-IA.`, ok: false, why: `It meets the time limit but costs several times more per GB than Glacier storage.` },
      { t: `Store the footage in S3 One Zone-IA.`, ok: false, why: `It is available instantly but costs about three times more per GB than Glacier Flexible Retrieval, and it lives in one AZ.` },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "b4-058", mod: 9, domain: "cost", type: "mcq",
    q: `A startup is building a three-tier app. The front end is a static website, the business logic is a containerized service, and data lives in a relational database. The team is small and wants simple deployments and low operating costs, with no servers to patch. Which solution meets these requirements?`,
    options: [
      { t: `Host the static site in Amazon S3 behind CloudFront, run the service on Amazon ECS with AWS Fargate, and use Amazon RDS for the database.`, ok: true, why: `S3, Fargate, and RDS are all managed, so nothing needs patching and costs follow usage.` },
      { t: `Host the static site on EC2 behind an ALB, run the service on Amazon ECS with EC2 instances, and run the database on EC2.`, ok: false, why: `All three tiers need servers the team must patch and scale.` },
      { t: `Host the static site in Amazon S3, run the service on Amazon EKS with self-managed EC2 worker nodes, and use Amazon RDS for PostgreSQL for the database.`, ok: false, why: `Self-managed EKS nodes add patching and the EKS control plane fee.` },
      { t: `Host the static site in Amazon S3, run the service on Amazon ECS with AWS Fargate, and store the data in Amazon DynamoDB.`, ok: false, why: `The data is relational, so DynamoDB would need a redesign.` },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "b4-059", mod: 10, domain: "resilient", type: "mcq",
    q: `A company needs shared storage for an application. It must be highly available, grow and shrink automatically with no minimum size, and work as a file system that many Linux servers can mount with a native protocol, both in AWS and in the on-premises data center. The data center connects to the VPC through a Site-to-Site VPN. Which storage solution meets these requirements?`,
    options: [
      { t: `Amazon EFS with mount targets in multiple Availability Zones`, ok: true, why: `EFS is elastic NFS storage that EC2 and on-premises Linux servers can mount over the VPN.` },
      { t: `An Amazon EBS volume with Multi-Attach enabled`, ok: false, why: `EBS is block storage in one AZ and can't be mounted from on premises.` },
      { t: `Amazon S3 mounted with Mountpoint for Amazon S3`, ok: false, why: `S3 is object storage. Mountpoint isn't a full POSIX file system for general shared use.` },
      { t: `Amazon FSx for Windows File Server with a Multi-AZ deployment type`, ok: false, why: `It uses SMB and needs a minimum size, so it isn't the native Linux choice with no minimum.` },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "b4-060", mod: 2, domain: "secure", type: "mcq",
    q: `A publishing group uses AWS Organizations with all features enabled. Its finance team says billing information in the member accounts must not be visible to anyone in those accounts, including each member account's root user. The company wants a central control that member account administrators can't override. Which solution meets these requirements?`,
    options: [
      { t: `Create a service control policy that denies the billing and cost management actions, and attach it to the organizational units that contain the member accounts.`, ok: true, why: `SCPs limit everyone in member accounts, including the root user.` },
      { t: `Create an IAM policy that denies billing actions and attach it to every IAM user and role in the member accounts.`, ok: false, why: `IAM policies don't restrict the root user of an account.` },
      { t: `Turn off IAM user and role access to billing in each member account's account settings.`, ok: false, why: `That setting doesn't affect the root user, who can still see billing.` },
      { t: `Remove the member accounts from the organization and use separate payment methods.`, ok: false, why: `It gives the member accounts full control of their own billing, the opposite of the goal.` },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "b4-061", mod: 8, domain: "resilient", type: "mcq",
    q: `An online grocer publishes order messages to an Amazon SNS topic. A subscription delivers them to an HTTPS endpoint in the on-premises warehouse system. The warehouse team found that some orders never arrived during network outages. The company must keep any message that can't be delivered and be able to analyze it for up to 14 days. Which solution meets these requirements with the <strong>LEAST</strong> development effort?`,
    options: [
      { t: `Attach an Amazon SQS dead-letter queue to the HTTPS subscription and set the queue's retention period to 14 days.`, ok: true, why: `SNS moves messages that exhaust delivery retries to the DLQ, and SQS can keep them for up to 14 days.` },
      { t: `Change the subscription to an AWS Lambda function that calls the warehouse endpoint, retries on failure, and writes any message that still fails to an Amazon DynamoDB table with a 14-day TTL.`, ok: false, why: `It works, but it's custom code that must be written and maintained.` },
      { t: `Add a second subscription that sends every message to an Amazon Kinesis Data Streams stream with 14-day retention, and compare the stream against warehouse records each day.`, ok: false, why: `It copies every message, not just failed ones, and needs a custom comparison job to find gaps.` },
      { t: `Turn on delivery status logging for the HTTPS protocol in Amazon CloudWatch Logs and set the log group retention to 14 days.`, ok: false, why: `Delivery logs record status, not the message bodies, so the failed orders can't be reprocessed or fully analyzed.` },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "b4-062", mod: 14, domain: "resilient", type: "mcq",
    q: `A gaming company stores player profiles and leaderboards in an Amazon DynamoDB table with provisioned capacity. It needs regular copies of the table data in an Amazon S3 bucket for analytics and long-term retention, with as little code as possible. Creating the copies must not affect the application's availability or use any of the table's read capacity units. Which solution meets these requirements?`,
    options: [
      { t: `Turn on point-in-time recovery for the table and use the DynamoDB export to Amazon S3 feature on a schedule.`, ok: true, why: `Exports read from the PITR backup data, so they consume no RCUs and don't affect the table.` },
      { t: `Use an AWS Glue job with the DynamoDB connector to scan the table every night and write the results to Amazon S3 as Parquet files.`, ok: false, why: `A scan reads through the table and uses its read capacity.` },
      { t: `Turn on DynamoDB Streams and have an AWS Lambda function write every change as an object to Amazon S3, then rebuild full copies from the changes with a separate job.`, ok: false, why: `It needs custom code to build full copies, which isn't minimal coding.` },
      { t: `Create on-demand backups of the table every night with AWS Backup and copy the recovery points to an Amazon S3 bucket.`, ok: false, why: `Backups stay in AWS Backup's format and can't be copied as data files into your own S3 bucket for analytics.` },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "b4-063", mod: 8, domain: "cost", type: "mcq",
    q: `A payments company is designing an asynchronous service that validates card transactions submitted by merchants. Each request must be processed at least once, order doesn't matter, and the data must be encrypted at rest. Volume is uneven, with quiet nights and busy afternoons. Which solution meets these requirements <strong>MOST</strong> cost-effectively?`,
    options: [
      { t: `Use an Amazon SQS standard queue with SQS-managed server-side encryption, and process messages with an AWS Lambda event source mapping.`, ok: true, why: `Standard queues give at-least-once delivery at the lowest price, SSE-SQS is free, and Lambda only bills when there is work.` },
      { t: `Use an Amazon SQS FIFO queue with server-side encryption using a customer managed AWS KMS key, and process messages with EC2 instances that poll the queue all day.`, ok: false, why: `FIFO and KMS calls cost more, ordering isn't needed, and always-on instances waste money at night.` },
      { t: `Use an Amazon Kinesis Data Streams stream with provisioned shards and server-side encryption, and process records with a fleet of EC2 consumers.`, ok: false, why: `Provisioned shards and consumers cost money even during quiet periods.` },
      { t: `Use Amazon MQ for RabbitMQ with an active/standby broker pair and encrypted storage, and process messages with containers on Amazon ECS.`, ok: false, why: `Brokers run all the time and cost more than SQS for a simple queue.` },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "b4-064", mod: 14, domain: "cost", type: "mcq",
    q: `A software company gives each engineering team its own AWS account for development. Some engineers keep launching very large EC2 instance types, and development spending is far over budget. The company wants to centrally restrict which EC2 instance types can be launched in all development accounts, now and as new accounts are added. Which solution meets these requirements with the <strong>LEAST</strong> development effort?`,
    options: [
      { t: `Put the development accounts in an OU in AWS Organizations and attach an SCP that denies ec2:RunInstances unless the instance type is on an approved list.`, ok: true, why: `One SCP on the OU applies to every current and future account in it, with no code.` },
      { t: `Create an Amazon EventBridge rule in each account that triggers an AWS Lambda function to stop any instance whose type isn't approved, and deploy the rule to new accounts with a script.`, ok: false, why: `It reacts after launch, needs code, and must be deployed to each account.` },
      { t: `Create an IAM policy in each account that denies large instance types, and attach it to every developer role.`, ok: false, why: `It must be repeated in every account, and account admins can change it.` },
      { t: `Use AWS Budgets in each account to email the team lead when spending passes 80% of the monthly budget.`, ok: false, why: `Alerts don't stop anyone from launching large instances.` },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "b4-065", mod: 13, domain: "performance", type: "multi",
    q: `A telecom provider records customer support calls in five languages and plans to add more. It has no staff to build or maintain machine learning models. Managers want written sentiment reports for every call, and all reports must be in English. Which combination of steps meets these requirements? (Choose three.)`,
    options: [
      { t: `Use Amazon Transcribe to turn each recording into text in its original language.`, ok: true, why: `Transcribe is managed speech-to-text with many supported languages.` },
      { t: `Use Amazon Translate to convert the transcripts into English.`, ok: true, why: `Translate is a managed translation service, so no models to maintain.` },
      { t: `Use Amazon Comprehend to run sentiment analysis on the English text and build the reports.`, ok: true, why: `Comprehend detects sentiment with pre-trained models.` },
      { t: `Use Amazon Lex to transcribe the recordings and detect the caller's intent.`, ok: false, why: `Lex builds chatbots for live conversations. It isn't for transcribing stored recordings.` },
      { t: `Use Amazon Polly to convert the transcripts into English audio for review.`, ok: false, why: `Polly turns text into speech, which doesn't help with translation or sentiment.` },
      { t: `Train a custom sentiment model in Amazon SageMaker for each language.`, ok: false, why: `The company can't maintain ML models, and managed services already cover this.` },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "b4-066", mod: 10, domain: "performance", type: "multi",
    q: `A company's on-premises applications use both NFS file shares and iSCSI block volumes, and local storage is almost full. The company wants to extend storage into AWS with low-latency local caching for frequently used data, and it can't change or re-architect the applications. Which combination of actions should a solutions architect take to meet these requirements? (Choose two.)`,
    options: [
      { t: `Deploy an AWS Storage Gateway S3 File Gateway to replace the NFS shares.`, ok: true, why: `File Gateway serves NFS locally with a cache and stores the files in Amazon S3.` },
      { t: `Deploy an AWS Storage Gateway Volume Gateway in cached mode to replace the block volumes.`, ok: true, why: `Cached volumes present iSCSI locally, keep hot data on site, and store the full volume in AWS.` },
      { t: `Deploy an AWS Storage Gateway Tape Gateway to replace the block volumes.`, ok: false, why: `Tape Gateway is for backup software that writes to virtual tapes, not live block storage.` },
      { t: `Move the NFS data to Amazon EFS and mount it from on premises over a Site-to-Site VPN.`, ok: false, why: `It works for NFS but has no local cache, so every read crosses the VPN.` },
      { t: `Use AWS DataSync to copy the block volumes into Amazon S3 every night.`, ok: false, why: `DataSync copies files and objects, and the applications would still need local block storage.` },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "b4-067", mod: 8, domain: "cost", type: "mcq",
    q: `A photo service stores high-resolution images in a versioned Amazon S3 bucket. Each edit uploads a new version of the same object key. The company only needs to keep the current version and one previous version of each image, but old versions now make up most of the bucket's cost. Which solution reduces the S3 costs with the <strong>LEAST</strong> operational overhead?`,
    options: [
      { t: `Add an S3 Lifecycle rule that expires noncurrent versions and keeps the 1 newest noncurrent version.`, ok: true, why: `The NewerNoncurrentVersions setting keeps the newest noncurrent version, so each image has two versions and older ones are deleted automatically.` },
      { t: `Write an AWS Lambda function that runs every night, lists every object's versions, and deletes all but the two newest versions of each image.`, ok: false, why: `It works but is custom code to write, run, and maintain.` },
      { t: `Turn off S3 Versioning on the bucket so that new uploads overwrite the existing object instead of creating new versions.`, ok: false, why: `Suspending versioning keeps all old versions and loses the previous version the company needs.` },
      { t: `Add an S3 Lifecycle rule that moves noncurrent versions to S3 Glacier Deep Archive after 1 day.`, ok: false, why: `It lowers the cost per GB but still stores every old version forever.` },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "b4-068", mod: 14, domain: "cost", type: "mcq",
    q: `A company pays for a dedicated 1 Gbps AWS Direct Connect connection, but monitoring shows average use is under 10% and peaks never pass 150 Mbps. The company wants to lower the cost of the link. Traffic must stay on a private connection and must not move to the public internet. Which solution meets these requirements?`,
    options: [
      { t: `Order a 200 Mbps hosted connection from an AWS Direct Connect Partner and move the traffic to it.`, ok: true, why: `A hosted connection at the needed speed costs less and is still private Direct Connect.` },
      { t: `Replace the Direct Connect connection with an AWS Site-to-Site VPN over the company's existing internet link to save on port-hour charges.`, ok: false, why: `It's cheaper but sends traffic over the public internet, which the company doesn't allow.` },
      { t: `Keep the dedicated connection and buy a Compute Savings Plan to discount the port-hour and data transfer charges.`, ok: false, why: `Savings Plans cover compute usage, not Direct Connect ports.` },
      { t: `Add a second 1 Gbps dedicated connection and use a link aggregation group to share the load.`, ok: false, why: `It doubles the port cost for capacity that isn't needed.` },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "b4-069", mod: 10, domain: "secure", type: "multi",
    q: `An architecture firm has several on-premises Windows file servers and wants to consolidate them into one Amazon FSx for Windows File Server file system. NTFS permissions on all files and folders must be preserved so access rights don't change. The office has a Site-to-Site VPN to the VPC. Which solutions meet these requirements? (Choose two.)`,
    options: [
      { t: `Deploy AWS DataSync agents on premises and run DataSync tasks that copy each share to the FSx file system.`, ok: true, why: `DataSync supports SMB sources and FSx for Windows targets and copies NTFS ACLs and metadata.` },
      { t: `Use Robocopy with the /COPYALL option from an on-premises server to copy each share to the FSx file system over the VPN.`, ok: true, why: `Robocopy over SMB copies data with its security descriptors, and AWS documents it for FSx for Windows migrations.` },
      { t: `Copy each share to an Amazon S3 bucket with the AWS CLI, then use DataSync to move the files from S3 to the FSx file system.`, ok: false, why: `S3 objects don't keep NTFS ACLs, so permissions are lost on the way.` },
      { t: `Copy the shares onto an AWS Snowball Edge device, import them into Amazon S3, and then copy them to the FSx file system.`, ok: false, why: `The S3 import step drops NTFS permissions.` },
      { t: `Use AWS Transfer Family with an SFTP endpoint to upload the shares to Amazon S3, then link the bucket to the FSx file system.`, ok: false, why: `SFTP and S3 don't carry NTFS ACLs, and FSx for Windows can't be linked to an S3 bucket.` },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "b4-070", mod: 13, domain: "performance", type: "mcq",
    q: `A payments company receives transaction events every few seconds. It must analyze the events in real time to flag suspicious patterns, and it must also land every event in its Amazon S3 data lake. Amazon Kinesis Data Analytics for SQL applications is no longer an option. Which solution meets these requirements with the <strong>MOST</strong> operational efficiency?`,
    options: [
      { t: `Ingest events into Amazon Kinesis Data Streams, analyze them with Amazon Managed Service for Apache Flink, and deliver the stream to Amazon S3 with Amazon Data Firehose.`, ok: true, why: `All three are managed. Flink does real-time analysis on the stream and Firehose lands the same events in S3.` },
      { t: `Ingest events with Amazon Data Firehose into Amazon S3, and run Amazon Athena queries every few minutes against the new objects to look for suspicious patterns as they arrive.`, ok: false, why: `Firehose buffers before writing and Athena runs on a schedule, so it isn't real-time analysis.` },
      { t: `Ingest events into Amazon SQS, process them with a fleet of EC2 instances running custom analysis code, and write results to S3.`, ok: false, why: `Custom code on EC2 is more to build and run than managed streaming services.` },
      { t: `Ingest events into Amazon MSK, run Apache Spark Streaming on a long-running Amazon EMR cluster, and write the output to S3.`, ok: false, why: `It works but means running Kafka and EMR clusters, which is more overhead.` },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "b4-071", mod: 6, domain: "resilient", type: "multi",
    q: `A magazine runs its content management system on a single EC2 instance with an Amazon Aurora MySQL Multi-AZ database. Article images are stored on an EBS volume attached to that instance. Readers complain about slow pages at peak times, and the site goes down whenever the instance fails. Which combination of actions should a solutions architect take to improve performance and resilience and meet these requirements? (Choose two.)`,
    options: [
      { t: `Move the images to an Amazon EFS file system mounted on every web instance.`, ok: true, why: `EFS lets many instances in several AZs share the same images.` },
      { t: `Create an AMI of the instance and run it in an Auto Scaling group across multiple Availability Zones behind an Application Load Balancer.`, ok: true, why: `More instances handle peaks, and the group replaces failed ones automatically.` },
      { t: `Move the images to an io2 EBS volume with Multi-Attach and attach it to new instances in other Availability Zones.`, ok: false, why: `Multi-Attach only works within one AZ, so it doesn't give multi-AZ resilience.` },
      { t: `Resize the instance to a larger type and add an Elastic IP so DNS doesn't change after a restart.`, ok: false, why: `A bigger single instance is still a single point of failure.` },
      { t: `Add an Aurora Replica in the same Availability Zone as the instance.`, ok: false, why: `The database is already Multi-AZ. The bottleneck is the single web instance.` },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "b4-072", mod: 11, domain: "secure", type: "mcq",
    q: `A SaaS company sells a cost-monitoring service. To work, it must call AWS APIs in each customer's account to describe EC2 instances and read CloudWatch metrics. Customers don't want to hand over long-term credentials, and the company wants to prevent a "confused deputy" where one customer tricks the service into reading another customer's account. What is the <strong>MOST</strong> secure way to get access?`,
    options: [
      { t: `Have each customer create an IAM role with read-only EC2 and CloudWatch permissions that trusts the company's account and requires a unique external ID.`, ok: true, why: `Roles give temporary credentials, and a per-customer external ID blocks the confused deputy problem.` },
      { t: `Have each customer create an IAM user with read-only EC2 and CloudWatch permissions and send the access keys to the company, which stores them in AWS Secrets Manager.`, ok: false, why: `It relies on long-term keys that can leak, even if they're stored safely.` },
      { t: `Have each customer create an IAM role with read-only EC2 and CloudWatch permissions that trusts the company's account, with no other conditions.`, ok: false, why: `Without an external ID, the service could be tricked into using another customer's role.` },
      { t: `Have each customer add the company's account to their organization in AWS Organizations so the company can assume OrganizationAccountAccessRole.`, ok: false, why: `That role has full admin access and joining an outside organization isn't realistic.` },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "b4-073", mod: 10, domain: "performance", type: "mcq",
    q: `A retailer has hundreds of AWS accounts, each with one or more VPCs in us-east-1. A central networking team owns its own account and must connect all the VPCs to each other and to the data center, with the fewest moving parts to manage over time. Which solution is the <strong>MOST</strong> operationally efficient?`,
    options: [
      { t: `Create a transit gateway in the networking account, share it with the other accounts through AWS RAM, and attach each VPC to it.`, ok: true, why: `One hub with shared access gives hub-and-spoke routing without a mesh of connections.` },
      { t: `Create VPC peering connections from every VPC to every other VPC across all accounts, and add routes for each peer in every route table.`, ok: false, why: `Hundreds of VPCs would need tens of thousands of peerings and routes.` },
      { t: `Create a Site-to-Site VPN from each VPC's virtual private gateway to a software router on an EC2 instance in the networking account.`, ok: false, why: `It adds a self-managed router that is a bottleneck and single point of failure.` },
      { t: `Create interface VPC endpoints in every VPC that point to AWS PrivateLink services in each other VPC.`, ok: false, why: `PrivateLink exposes individual services, not full routing between networks.` },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "b4-074", mod: 14, domain: "cost", type: "mcq",
    q: `A company runs nightly data-processing jobs on EC2 instances in an Auto Scaling group with On-Demand pricing. The jobs run between 1:00 AM and 5:00 AM. If an instance disappears mid-job, another instance picks the job up and reruns it without problems. Which solution provides EC2 capacity for these jobs <strong>MOST</strong> cost-effectively?`,
    options: [
      { t: `Use Spot Instances in the Auto Scaling group.`, ok: true, why: `The jobs tolerate interruptions, so Spot gives the biggest discount.` },
      { t: `Buy a 1-year Standard Reserved Instance for each instance in the Auto Scaling group and keep them running all day.`, ok: false, why: `It pays for 24 hours a day of capacity that is only used for 4.` },
      { t: `Buy a 1-year Compute Savings Plan that covers the peak hourly spend of the nightly jobs.`, ok: false, why: `The commitment is paid every hour, so 20 idle hours a day waste most of it.` },
      { t: `Keep On-Demand Instances and create a scheduled scaling action that starts them at 1:00 AM and stops them at 5:00 AM.`, ok: false, why: `Scheduling helps but On-Demand still costs much more than Spot for interruption-tolerant work.` },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "b4-075", mod: 8, domain: "performance", type: "mcq",
    q: `A social app is adding photo uploads. Traffic spikes sharply during live events, and the company doesn't want the web servers to become a bottleneck when millions of users upload at once. Photos must end up in Amazon S3, and only signed-in users may upload. Which solution meets these requirements with the <strong>MOST</strong> scalability?`,
    options: [
      { t: `Have the app generate S3 presigned URLs for signed-in users and upload photos straight from the browser to S3.`, ok: true, why: `Uploads go directly to S3, which scales on its own, and the URL limits who can upload and for how long.` },
      { t: `Upload photos to the web servers, which save them to an Amazon EFS file system and copy them to S3 with a background job every few minutes.`, ok: false, why: `Every upload still passes through the web servers, which become the bottleneck.` },
      { t: `Upload photos to the web servers, which forward them to S3, and add more instances to the Auto Scaling group during events.`, ok: false, why: `It scales, but the servers carry all the upload traffic and cost.` },
      { t: `Upload photos to an Amazon API Gateway REST API that passes the file body to AWS Lambda, which writes it to S3.`, ok: false, why: `API Gateway limits payloads to 10 MB, so high-resolution uploads can fail.` },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "b4-076", mod: 10, domain: "performance", type: "mcq",
    q: `A travel booking site keeps its reservation database in one North American data center and wants to serve a global audience from several AWS Regions. Each Region will run its own web tier. The company must keep a single primary reservation database, and changes must reach every Region within 1 second. Which solution should a solutions architect recommend to meet these requirements?`,
    options: [
      { t: `Migrate the database to an Amazon Aurora global database with the primary cluster in North America and secondary clusters in the other Regions, and turn on write forwarding.`, ok: true, why: `Aurora global databases keep one writer and replicate to other Regions with typical lag under a second.` },
      { t: `Rewrite the application to use Amazon DynamoDB global tables, and have each Region read and write to its local replica table.`, ok: false, why: `Global tables are multi-active, not a single primary, and need an application rewrite.` },
      { t: `Migrate to Amazon RDS for MySQL in North America and create cross-Region read replicas in each Region.`, ok: false, why: `Cross-Region replicas use asynchronous binlog replication, so lag isn't held under a second.` },
      { t: `Run a separate Amazon RDS database in each Region and sync them every hour with AWS DMS.`, ok: false, why: `There would be several primaries, and hourly sync misses the 1-second target.` },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "b4-077", mod: 11, domain: "performance", type: "mcq",
    q: `An image-processing app has an ALB in public subnets and EC2 instances in private subnets across two Availability Zones. Users say the app is slow. Logs show millions of junk requests from a handful of fixed IP addresses. The company needs a fast, temporary fix while it designs a long-term solution. What should a solutions architect recommend to meet these requirements?`,
    options: [
      { t: `Add inbound deny rules for those IP addresses to the network ACLs of the public subnets.`, ok: true, why: `A few fixed IPs can be blocked right away with NACL deny rules, before traffic reaches the ALB.` },
      { t: `Subscribe to AWS Shield Advanced, enable it on the load balancer, and open a case with the Shield Response Team to analyze the traffic.`, ok: false, why: `It takes time to onboard and costs a monthly fee, which isn't a quick fix for a few known IPs.` },
      { t: `Increase the Auto Scaling group's maximum size so the instances can absorb the extra requests until the investigation ends.`, ok: false, why: `It pays to serve junk traffic and doesn't stop it.` },
      { t: `Put an Amazon CloudFront distribution in front of the ALB and cache all responses for one hour.`, ok: false, why: `Dynamic image processing can't be cached for an hour, and the junk requests still reach the origin.` },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "b4-078", mod: 3, domain: "secure", type: "mcq",
    q: `A marketing company runs applications in a VPC in eu-west-1 (10.10.0.0/16) that must connect privately to databases in a VPC in ap-southeast-2 (10.20.0.0/16). The traffic must not cross the public internet, and the databases' security group must allow only traffic from the application VPC. The team wants a simple design with no extra appliances. Which network design meets these requirements?`,
    options: [
      { t: `Create an inter-Region VPC peering connection, add routes in both VPCs' route tables, and allow 10.10.0.0/16 in the database security group.`, ok: true, why: `Peering works across Regions, and cross-Region rules must use CIDR blocks.` },
      { t: `Create an inter-Region VPC peering connection, add routes in both VPCs' route tables, and allow the application servers' security group ID in the database security group.`, ok: false, why: `Security group references don't work across Regions in a peering connection.` },
      { t: `Create a gateway VPC endpoint in each VPC and allow the endpoint's prefix list in the database security group.`, ok: false, why: `Gateway endpoints only reach S3 and DynamoDB, not other VPCs.` },
      { t: `Give the databases public IP addresses in public subnets and allow the eu-west-1 NAT gateway's Elastic IP in the database security group.`, ok: false, why: `It exposes the databases to the internet instead of a private path.` },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "b4-079", mod: 8, domain: "cost", type: "mcq",
    q: `A software company gives each of its 30 developers a PostgreSQL database for development. Each database is busy for only about 4 hours of the 8-hour workday, sits idle the rest of the day, and is unused at night and on weekends. Developers don't want to start or stop anything by hand, and a short delay on the first query after a quiet period is fine. Which solution meets these requirements <strong>MOST</strong> cost-effectively?`,
    options: [
      { t: `Give each developer an Amazon Aurora PostgreSQL Serverless v2 cluster with a minimum capacity of 0 ACUs so it pauses when idle.`, ok: true, why: `It scales with use and pauses automatically when idle, so idle time costs almost nothing, with no manual steps.` },
      { t: `Give each developer an Amazon RDS for PostgreSQL DB instance and have an AWS Lambda function stop it at 6 PM and start it at 8 AM.`, ok: false, why: `Instances still bill for the idle hours of the workday, and schedules need maintenance.` },
      { t: `Give each developer an Amazon RDS for PostgreSQL DB instance on a 1-year reserved instance.`, ok: false, why: `Reservations pay for 24/7 capacity that's idle most of the time.` },
      { t: `Give each developer an Amazon Aurora PostgreSQL provisioned cluster with a small instance class running all the time.`, ok: false, why: `Provisioned clusters bill every hour, idle or not.` },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "b4-080", mod: 14, domain: "resilient", type: "mcq",
    q: `A company uses AWS Organizations and AWS Backup. An audit found that many resources aren't covered by any backup plan because they were never tagged for backup. The company must back up all of its supported AWS resources from now on, including new ones, without relying on people to tag them. Which solution meets these requirements with the <strong>LEAST</strong> operational overhead?`,
    options: [
      { t: `Set the backup plan's resource assignment to include all supported resource types.`, ok: true, why: `AWS Backup can assign every supported resource type in the account, so new resources are covered without tags.` },
      { t: `Use AWS Config to find untagged resources every day, tag them with a Lambda function, and select the tag in the backup plan.`, ok: false, why: `It works but adds a rule, a function, and tagging logic to maintain.` },
      { t: `Ask every resource owner to review their resources each month and add the backup tag to anything new.`, ok: false, why: `It relies on people, which is how resources were missed in the first place.` },
      { t: `Create a separate backup plan for each resource as it's created, using AWS CloudFormation hooks.`, ok: false, why: `One plan per resource is a lot of work and hard to manage.` },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "b4-081", mod: 8, domain: "performance", type: "mcq",
    q: `A recipe-sharing site lets users upload food photos. Each photo must be resized automatically into several sizes for phones, tablets, and desktops. Traffic is unpredictable, with long quiet periods and sudden viral spikes. The company wants a highly available design that scales as far as possible without capacity planning. What should a solutions architect do to meet these requirements?`,
    options: [
      { t: `Store uploads in an S3 bucket and use S3 event notifications to invoke an AWS Lambda function that writes the resized images to S3.`, ok: true, why: `S3 and Lambda scale automatically per upload and are highly available with no servers to size.` },
      { t: `Store uploads on an Amazon EFS file system and run a resizing service on EC2 instances in an Auto Scaling group that scales on CPU utilization across two Availability Zones.`, ok: false, why: `It works but needs instance sizing and scaling tuning, and it scales more slowly for sudden spikes.` },
      { t: `Store uploads in an S3 bucket and run a resizing script on a single large EC2 instance that polls the bucket every minute.`, ok: false, why: `A single instance is a bottleneck and a single point of failure.` },
      { t: `Store uploads in an Amazon RDS database as binary objects and resize them with a stored procedure triggered on insert.`, ok: false, why: `Databases are a poor place for images, and the database becomes the scaling limit.` },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "b4-082", mod: 8, domain: "secure", type: "mcq",
    q: `A company is moving microservices to Amazon EKS. Compliance requires the cluster API endpoint to be private only, and worker nodes must run in private subnets that have no route to a NAT gateway or internet gateway. New managed nodes fail to join the cluster because they can't pull images or call required AWS APIs. Which solution allows the nodes to join the cluster and meets these requirements?`,
    options: [
      { t: `Create interface VPC endpoints for Amazon ECR (api and dkr), EC2, and AWS STS, plus a gateway endpoint for Amazon S3, in the cluster VPC.`, ok: true, why: `Private clusters without internet access need these endpoints so nodes can pull images and get credentials.` },
      { t: `Move the worker nodes to public subnets with public IP addresses so they can reach the AWS service endpoints over the internet.`, ok: false, why: `Compliance requires nodes in private subnets.` },
      { t: `Turn on public access for the cluster API endpoint and restrict it to the company's office IP ranges.`, ok: false, why: `Compliance requires the endpoint to stay private only.` },
      { t: `Add an outbound rule to the node security group that allows all traffic to 0.0.0.0/0.`, ok: false, why: `With no route out of the VPC, opening the security group doesn't give the nodes a path to the services.` },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "b4-083", mod: 8, domain: "performance", type: "mcq",
    q: `A brokerage offers an API that customers call to fetch account balances. Traffic jumps sharply around tax season, and customers expect consistently low response times, including for the first request after a quiet period. The company wants a managed compute layer behind the API with as little to operate as possible. Which solution meets these requirements with the <strong>LEAST</strong> operational overhead?`,
    options: [
      { t: `Use Amazon API Gateway with AWS Lambda functions that have provisioned concurrency.`, ok: true, why: `Provisioned concurrency keeps functions initialized, avoiding cold starts, and there are no servers to run.` },
      { t: `Use an Application Load Balancer in front of an Amazon EC2 Auto Scaling group with scheduled scaling actions for tax season and target tracking at other times.`, ok: false, why: `It gives steady latency but means managing instances, AMIs, and scaling settings.` },
      { t: `Use Amazon API Gateway with AWS Lambda functions that run with the default on-demand concurrency.`, ok: false, why: `Cold starts can add latency on the first requests after a quiet period.` },
      { t: `Use an Application Load Balancer in front of an Amazon EKS cluster with the Cluster Autoscaler and Horizontal Pod Autoscaler configured.`, ok: false, why: `Running EKS is much more to operate than Lambda for an API like this.` },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "b4-084", mod: 12, domain: "secure", type: "mcq",
    q: `A company's administrators use AWS Systems Manager Session Manager to reach EC2 instances instead of SSH. The audit team wants a record of every session's commands and output kept in an Amazon S3 bucket for archiving. The company wants the simplest setup and doesn't want to install extra software on the instances. Which solution meets this requirement with the <strong>MOST</strong> operational efficiency?`,
    options: [
      { t: `Turn on S3 logging in the Session Manager preferences and choose the archive bucket.`, ok: true, why: `Session Manager can stream session logs straight to S3 with a single setting.` },
      { t: `Install the Amazon CloudWatch agent on every instance to collect shell history into a log group, and create an export task that copies the log group to S3 every day.`, ok: false, why: `It needs agents and daily exports, and shell history doesn't capture command output.` },
      { t: `Turn on CloudWatch Logs streaming in the Session Manager preferences, and create an Amazon Data Firehose subscription that delivers the log group to S3.`, ok: false, why: `It works but adds a log group and a delivery stream that aren't needed.` },
      { t: `Turn on AWS CloudTrail data events for Systems Manager and deliver the trail to the archive bucket.`, ok: false, why: `CloudTrail records session start and end API calls, not the commands typed and their output.` },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "b4-085", mod: 12, domain: "resilient", type: "mcq",
    q: `An application uses an Amazon RDS for MySQL DB instance whose storage is almost full, and growth is hard to predict. The team wants more disk space now and in the future without downtime, and without someone watching free storage every week. Which solution meets these requirements with the <strong>LEAST</strong> effort?`,
    options: [
      { t: `Turn on RDS storage autoscaling with a maximum storage threshold.`, ok: true, why: `RDS grows storage automatically when free space runs low, with no downtime.` },
      { t: `Create a CloudWatch alarm on FreeStorageSpace that notifies the team, which then increases the allocated storage in the console.`, ok: false, why: `It works but someone must act every time the alarm fires.` },
      { t: `Restore the latest snapshot to a new DB instance with larger storage and point the application at the new endpoint.`, ok: false, why: `Switching endpoints causes downtime and has to be repeated as data grows.` },
      { t: `Migrate the data to an EC2-hosted MySQL server with an EBS volume that the team resizes with Elastic Volumes when needed.`, ok: false, why: `It moves database admin work onto the team and still needs manual resizing.` },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "b4-086", mod: 12, domain: "secure", type: "mcq",
    q: `A consulting firm builds data-gathering and analytics toolkits on AWS for clients around the world. It wants to publish a central, approved set of these toolkits that client teams can launch themselves in their own accounts, with consistent configuration and version control. Clients should only be able to launch what the firm has approved. Which solution meets these requirements?`,
    options: [
      { t: `Create AWS Service Catalog products from the toolkits and share the portfolio with the client accounts.`, ok: true, why: `Service Catalog publishes approved, versioned products that users launch self-service.` },
      { t: `Store AWS CloudFormation templates in an S3 bucket and email clients links with instructions for deploying them in their accounts.`, ok: false, why: `It has no central catalog, version control, or launch constraints.` },
      { t: `Use AWS Systems Manager documents to run the toolkit installers on client EC2 instances from the firm's account.`, ok: false, why: `It runs commands on instances, not a self-service catalog of complete solutions.` },
      { t: `Package the toolkits as AMIs, share them with each client account, and let clients build the rest of the environment by hand.`, ok: false, why: `AMIs only cover instances, and manual building isn't consistent.` },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "b4-087", mod: 6, domain: "cost", type: "mcq",
    q: `A startup is launching a new web app on EC2 that stores data in Amazon DynamoDB. Traffic is unpredictable and could swing from near zero to heavy within minutes. Read and write volume will be moderate to high, and the table must scale with traffic without the team planning capacity. Which table configuration meets these requirements <strong>MOST</strong> cost-effectively?`,
    options: [
      { t: `On-demand capacity mode with the DynamoDB Standard table class.`, ok: true, why: `On-demand scales instantly with traffic and bills per request, and Standard suits a busy table.` },
      { t: `Provisioned capacity mode with auto scaling and the DynamoDB Standard-Infrequent Access table class.`, ok: false, why: `Auto scaling lags behind sudden spikes, and Standard-IA raises request prices on a busy table.` },
      { t: `On-demand capacity mode with the DynamoDB Standard-Infrequent Access table class.`, ok: false, why: `Standard-IA lowers storage cost but raises read and write prices, which hurts a high-traffic table.` },
      { t: `Provisioned capacity mode sized for the expected peak, with reserved capacity purchased for one year.`, ok: false, why: `It pays for peak capacity all the time, even when traffic is near zero.` },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "b4-088", mod: 11, domain: "secure", type: "mcq",
    q: `A retail group's brands each manage their own AWS account in one organization, and each keeps stock levels in a DynamoDB table in its account. A new reporting application in a shared account must read items from every brand's table. Security wants least privilege and no long-term credentials. Which authentication option meets these requirements in the <strong>MOST</strong> secure way?`,
    options: [
      { t: `In each brand account, create a role with read access to its table that trusts only the reporting app's role. Give the app role permission to assume those roles.`, ok: true, why: `Cross-account roles give short-lived credentials, and each trust is limited to one specific role.` },
      { t: `In each brand account, create an IAM user with read access to its table, store the user's access keys in AWS Secrets Manager in the shared account, and rotate them every 90 days.`, ok: false, why: `It still uses long-term keys, even if they're stored and rotated.` },
      { t: `In each brand account, create a role with DynamoDB full access that trusts the entire shared account, and let the reporting app assume it.`, ok: false, why: `Full access and trusting the whole account break least privilege.` },
      { t: `Add a resource-based policy to each brand's table that allows any principal in the organization to read it, using the aws:PrincipalOrgID condition.`, ok: false, why: `It lets every principal in the organization read the tables, not just the reporting app.` },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "b4-089", mod: 12, domain: "performance", type: "multi",
    q: `A company runs container workloads on Amazon EKS. Load changes a lot during the day, and the company wants both the number of pods and the number of worker nodes to grow and shrink with demand, using as little custom work as possible. Which combination of steps meets these requirements with the <strong>LEAST</strong> operational overhead? (Choose two.)`,
    options: [
      { t: `Install the Kubernetes Metrics Server and use the Horizontal Pod Autoscaler to scale pods.`, ok: true, why: `HPA scales pods on CPU or memory using metrics from the Metrics Server.` },
      { t: `Use Karpenter or the Kubernetes Cluster Autoscaler to add and remove nodes as pods need capacity.`, ok: true, why: `Node autoscalers add nodes when pods can't be scheduled and remove empty ones.` },
      { t: `Use an AWS Lambda function on an EventBridge schedule to change the node group's desired size every hour.`, ok: false, why: `Fixed schedules don't follow real demand and are custom code.` },
      { t: `Use the Vertical Pod Autoscaler only, so each pod gets bigger as load rises.`, ok: false, why: `VPA changes pod size but doesn't add nodes, and pods restart to resize.` },
      { t: `Create a CloudWatch alarm on cluster CPU that emails the team to scale the node group manually.`, ok: false, why: `Manual scaling is slow and adds work.` },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "b4-090", mod: 13, domain: "secure", type: "mcq",
    q: `A company's developers keep hitting AccessDenied and UnauthorizedOperation errors and want to find which principals and actions cause them. AWS CloudTrail already delivers logs from all accounts to an Amazon S3 bucket. The team wants to run ad hoc searches over months of logs without building a pipeline. Which solution meets these requirements with the <strong>LEAST</strong> effort?`,
    options: [
      { t: `Create an Amazon Athena table over the CloudTrail logs and query for those error codes.`, ok: true, why: `Athena queries the logs in place with SQL, with no servers or pipeline.` },
      { t: `Load the CloudTrail logs into an Amazon Redshift cluster every night with a COPY job, and run SQL queries against the loaded tables.`, ok: false, why: `It works but means running a cluster and a nightly load.` },
      { t: `Download the log files to a laptop and search them with grep.`, ok: false, why: `Months of logs across accounts are too big to search by hand.` },
      { t: `Stream the logs into an Amazon OpenSearch Service domain with a Lambda function and build dashboards for the errors.`, ok: false, why: `It needs a domain, ingestion code, and dashboards, which is more effort.` },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "b4-091", mod: 14, domain: "cost", type: "mcq",
    q: `A company wants to show its AWS costs inside an internal operations dashboard that it built itself. The dashboard must pull cost data programmatically for the current year and show a cost forecast for the next 12 months. The team wants the simplest solution with nothing extra to run. Which solution meets these requirements with the <strong>LEAST</strong> operational overhead?`,
    options: [
      { t: `Call the AWS Cost Explorer API for cost and usage data and its forecast operation for the next 12 months.`, ok: true, why: `The Cost Explorer API returns historical costs and forecasts directly.` },
      { t: `Export the AWS Cost and Usage Report to Amazon S3, query it with Amazon Athena, and build a forecasting model in Amazon SageMaker for the next 12 months.`, ok: false, why: `It works but needs a pipeline and a custom forecast model.` },
      { t: `Download monthly invoices from the Billing console and enter the totals into the dashboard by hand.`, ok: false, why: `It's manual and has no forecast.` },
      { t: `Use AWS Budgets reports emailed every day and parse the emails with a script that updates the dashboard.`, ok: false, why: `Parsing emails is fragile, and budget reports don't give a 12-month forecast feed.` },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "b4-092", mod: 9, domain: "resilient", type: "mcq",
    q: `A database administrator triggered a manual failover of an Amazon Aurora PostgreSQL writer during a scaling change, and the application was down for about 3 minutes while it reconnected. The company does this kind of change regularly and wants to reduce the downtime with minimal changes to the application. Which solution meets these requirements with the <strong>LEAST</strong> operational overhead?`,
    options: [
      { t: `Put Amazon RDS Proxy in front of the cluster and point the application at the proxy endpoint.`, ok: true, why: `RDS Proxy keeps client connections open and reroutes to the new writer, cutting failover downtime.` },
      { t: `Add more Aurora Replicas in other Availability Zones and lower the failover priority tier of the current writer.`, ok: false, why: `More replicas don't stop clients from dropping connections and waiting on DNS during failover.` },
      { t: `Lower the TTL of the cluster endpoint's DNS record in Route 53 and add retry logic with backoff throughout the application code.`, ok: false, why: `The cluster endpoint DNS is managed by AWS, and retries mean code changes everywhere.` },
      { t: `Run the scaling changes from an AWS Lambda function during a nightly maintenance window.`, ok: false, why: `It moves the downtime to another time but doesn't reduce it.` },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "b4-093", mod: 12, domain: "resilient", type: "mcq",
    q: `A streaming service runs in one Region with EC2 web and app tiers in Auto Scaling groups behind load balancers and an Amazon Aurora MySQL cluster. The company is expanding globally and must keep the service running even if an entire Region fails, with minimal downtime and data loss. Which solution provides the <strong>MOST</strong> fault tolerance?`,
    options: [
      { t: `Deploy both tiers in a second Region, convert the cluster to an Aurora global database, and use Route 53 failover routing with health checks.`, ok: true, why: `A running second stack, sub-second replication, and DNS failover keep downtime and data loss low.` },
      { t: `Deploy both tiers in a second Region and create a cross-Region Aurora read replica, then switch DNS by hand and promote the replica when the primary Region fails.`, ok: false, why: `Manual promotion and DNS changes add downtime compared with an Aurora global database and health checks.` },
      { t: `Spread the Auto Scaling groups across all Availability Zones in the current Region and add more Aurora Replicas there.`, ok: false, why: `It survives an AZ failure but not a Region failure.` },
      { t: `Take Aurora snapshots every hour, copy them to a second Region, and use AWS CloudFormation to build the stack there during an outage.`, ok: false, why: `Rebuilding takes a long time and loses up to an hour of data.` },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "b4-094", mod: 14, domain: "secure", type: "mcq",
    q: `A healthcare company is moving its relational databases, which hold transaction data and patient details, from its own servers to AWS. It wants to improve security and hand off as much database administration as possible, including patching and backups. The company doesn't want to rewrite its applications. Which solution meets these requirements?`,
    options: [
      { t: `Migrate the databases to Amazon RDS with encryption at rest using AWS KMS.`, ok: true, why: `RDS takes over patching and backups, and KMS encryption protects the stored data.` },
      { t: `Migrate the databases to Amazon EC2 instances with encrypted EBS volumes, and manage patching and backups with AWS Systems Manager and AWS Backup.`, ok: false, why: `It's secure but leaves database administration with the company.` },
      { t: `Migrate the databases to Amazon DynamoDB with encryption at rest.`, ok: false, why: `Moving relational data to DynamoDB needs an application rewrite.` },
      { t: `Store database dump files in Amazon S3 with SSE-S3 and restore them on demand to an EC2 database server.`, ok: false, why: `It isn't a live database and adds work.` },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "b4-095", mod: 11, domain: "performance", type: "mcq",
    q: `A data company subscribes to a third-party feed that sends an HTTPS webhook whenever new data is ready. A developer has written an AWS Lambda function that downloads the data when called. The function needs a public HTTPS endpoint that the third party can call, and the team wants the simplest setup with nothing else to manage. Which solution meets these requirements with the <strong>MOST</strong> operational efficiency?`,
    options: [
      { t: `Create a Lambda function URL and give it to the third party as the webhook address.`, ok: true, why: `A function URL is a built-in HTTPS endpoint for one function, with no extra service to configure.` },
      { t: `Create an Amazon API Gateway REST API with a resource and POST method that integrates with the function, deploy it to a stage, and share the stage URL.`, ok: false, why: `It works, but it's more to build and manage than a function URL for one webhook.` },
      { t: `Put an Application Load Balancer in front of the function with an HTTPS listener and an ACM certificate.`, ok: false, why: `An ALB costs money every hour and is more to manage for a single webhook.` },
      { t: `Ask the third party to publish to an Amazon SNS topic that invokes the function.`, ok: false, why: `The third party sends webhooks, not SNS API calls, and would need AWS credentials.` },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "b4-096", mod: 11, domain: "secure", type: "multi",
    q: `A SaaS company exposes a Regional Amazon API Gateway REST API and uses Route 53 for DNS. It wants every customer to get their own HTTPS URL, such as acme.api.example.com, without creating a new resource for each customer. Which combination of steps meets these requirements with the <strong>MOST</strong> operational efficiency? (Choose three.)`,
    options: [
      { t: `Create a wildcard record (*.api.example.com) in the Route 53 hosted zone that points to the API Gateway custom domain.`, ok: true, why: `One wildcard record sends every customer subdomain to the API.` },
      { t: `Request a wildcard certificate for *.api.example.com in AWS Certificate Manager in the same Region as the API.`, ok: true, why: `Regional custom domains need an ACM certificate in the same Region, and a wildcard covers all customers.` },
      { t: `Create a wildcard custom domain name in API Gateway that uses the certificate and maps to the API.`, ok: true, why: `API Gateway supports wildcard custom domains, so all subdomains reach one API.` },
      { t: `Request a separate ACM certificate for each customer subdomain in us-east-1.`, ok: false, why: `A wildcard certificate avoids per-customer certificates, and Regional APIs need the certificate in their own Region.` },
      { t: `Create a separate hosted zone for each customer and delegate it from the parent zone.`, ok: false, why: `Per-customer hosted zones add cost and work that a wildcard record avoids.` },
      { t: `Deploy a separate API Gateway stage for each customer.`, ok: false, why: `Per-customer stages are a lot of work and aren't needed for unique URLs.` },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "b4-097", mod: 13, domain: "secure", type: "mcq",
    q: `A fintech's policy says its Amazon S3 buckets must not hold personally identifiable information. A recent check found PII in several buckets. The company needs automatic, ongoing detection of PII in S3 and an alert to the security team's email whenever new sensitive data is found. Which solution meets these requirements?`,
    options: [
      { t: `Turn on Amazon Macie automated sensitive data discovery, and send its sensitive data findings through an Amazon EventBridge rule to an Amazon SNS topic that emails the team.`, ok: true, why: `Macie finds PII in S3, and EventBridge plus SNS turn findings into emails.` },
      { t: `Turn on Amazon GuardDuty S3 Protection and send its findings through an Amazon EventBridge rule to an Amazon SNS topic that emails the team.`, ok: false, why: `GuardDuty looks for threats and suspicious access, not PII inside objects.` },
      { t: `Turn on Amazon Inspector for the account and email its findings to the team.`, ok: false, why: `Inspector scans workloads for vulnerabilities, not data in S3.` },
      { t: `Run Amazon Comprehend PII detection on every object with a nightly AWS Lambda job and email a summary.`, ok: false, why: `It's custom code that Macie already provides as a managed feature.` },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "b4-098", mod: 5, domain: "cost", type: "mcq",
    q: `A company keeps VPC flow logs and CloudTrail logs from all accounts in one S3 bucket in a logging account. Logs must be immediately available for frequent analysis for the first 30 days, kept as a backup for the next 60 days, and deleted 90 days after creation. Logs are combined into compressed files of about 100 MB each, and backup logs are almost never read. Which solution meets these requirements <strong>MOST</strong> cost-effectively?`,
    options: [
      { t: `Keep objects in S3 Standard, move them to S3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval 30 days after creation, and expire them 90 days after creation.`, ok: true, why: `Glacier storage is cheap enough that even with its 90-day minimum charge, it beats Standard-IA for 60 days.` },
      { t: `Keep objects in S3 Standard, move them to S3 Standard-IA 30 days after creation, and expire them 90 days after creation.`, ok: false, why: `Standard-IA costs several times more per GB than Glacier for data that is almost never read.` },
      { t: `Keep objects in S3 Standard for the full 90 days, then expire them.`, ok: false, why: `Paying Standard rates for 60 days of rarely read backups costs the most.` },
      { t: `Keep objects in S3 One Zone-IA from creation, then expire them 90 days after creation.`, ok: false, why: `One Zone-IA isn't highly available for the first 30 days, and it costs more than Glacier for the backup period.` },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "b4-099", mod: 11, domain: "secure", type: "mcq",
    q: `A company is creating an Amazon EKS cluster. Its security policy says all Kubernetes secrets stored in etcd must be encrypted with a key that the security team creates, controls, and can audit or disable. The platform team wants to use a managed EKS feature rather than run extra tooling inside the cluster. Which solution meets these requirements?`,
    options: [
      { t: `Create a customer managed AWS KMS key and turn on EKS envelope encryption of secrets with that key.`, ok: true, why: `EKS can encrypt secrets in etcd with your own KMS key, which you control and audit through CloudTrail.` },
      { t: `Rely on the default EKS encryption of Kubernetes API data with an AWS owned key.`, ok: false, why: `An AWS owned key isn't controlled, audited, or disabled by the security team.` },
      { t: `Store the secrets in AWS Secrets Manager instead and encrypt the worker nodes' EBS volumes with a KMS key.`, ok: false, why: `Encrypting node volumes doesn't encrypt secrets stored in etcd on the control plane.` },
      { t: `Install the Amazon EBS CSI driver and turn on encryption for persistent volumes with a customer managed KMS key.`, ok: false, why: `It encrypts pod volumes, not Kubernetes secrets in etcd.` },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "b4-100", mod: 6, domain: "cost", type: "mcq",
    q: `A logistics company runs its production Amazon RDS for PostgreSQL database as a Single-AZ instance. Data scientists now need near real-time, read-only access for heavy queries that must not slow production. The company also wants the database to be highly available, and it wants to keep the number of DB instances it pays for as low as possible. Which solution meets these requirements <strong>MOST</strong> cost-effectively?`,
    options: [
      { t: `Convert the database to a Multi-AZ DB cluster deployment and point the data scientists at the reader endpoint.`, ok: true, why: `A Multi-AZ DB cluster has two readable standbys, so it gives high availability and read capacity in one deployment.` },
      { t: `Convert to a Multi-AZ DB instance deployment and add two read replicas for the data scientists.`, ok: false, why: `It needs four instances in total, since the standby can't serve reads, so it costs more.` },
      { t: `Keep the Single-AZ instance and add one read replica in another AZ for the data scientists.`, ok: false, why: `It isn't highly available because failover to a replica is manual.` },
      { t: `Scale the Single-AZ instance up to a larger class and let the data scientists query it directly.`, ok: false, why: `Heavy queries still hit production, and one instance isn't highly available.` },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "b4-101", mod: 6, domain: "resilient", type: "mcq",
    q: `A ticketing company runs a three-tier app across three Availability Zones. An ALB fronts one EC2 web server that keeps user session state in memory, and the database is MySQL on a single EC2 instance. The company expects sudden traffic surges and needs the app to scale and stay highly available across all three Availability Zones. Which solution meets these requirements?`,
    options: [
      { t: `Move MySQL to an Amazon RDS Multi-AZ DB cluster, store sessions in a highly available ElastiCache for Redis OSS cluster, and run the web tier in an Auto Scaling group across three AZs.`, ok: true, why: `Sessions leave the servers so any instance can serve a user, the web tier scales across AZs, and the DB cluster fails over automatically.` },
      { t: `Move MySQL to Amazon RDS Multi-AZ, keep sessions in memory on the web servers with ALB sticky sessions, and run the web tier in an Auto Scaling group across three AZs.`, ok: false, why: `If an instance is replaced or scaled in, its users lose their sessions.` },
      { t: `Keep MySQL on EC2 but take hourly snapshots, store sessions in ElastiCache for Memcached, and run the web tier in an Auto Scaling group across three AZs.`, ok: false, why: `The database is still one instance, and Memcached has no replication, so sessions are lost if a node fails.` },
      { t: `Move MySQL to a Single-AZ Amazon RDS instance, store sessions in Amazon DynamoDB, and move the web server to a larger instance type.`, ok: false, why: `One web server and a Single-AZ database aren't highly available.` },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "b4-102", mod: 11, domain: "secure", type: "mcq",
    q: `A video streaming company serves its catalog through Amazon CloudFront. Licensing deals mean new shows launch country by country. Viewers in countries where a show isn't licensed yet must not be able to watch it, and they should see a friendly message instead of a raw error. Which solution meets these requirements?`,
    options: [
      { t: `Turn on CloudFront geographic restrictions with an allow list of licensed countries, and set a custom error page for 403 responses.`, ok: true, why: `Geo restriction blocks other countries at the edge, and a custom error page shows the friendly message.` },
      { t: `Create a Route 53 geolocation routing policy that sends unlicensed countries to a different origin with an error page.`, ok: false, why: `Users could still reach the CloudFront domain directly, and DNS location can be wrong with public resolvers.` },
      { t: `Issue CloudFront signed URLs only to viewers whose billing address is in a licensed country.`, ok: false, why: `A billing address doesn't stop someone from watching from another country.` },
      { t: `Add an AWS WAF rule on the distribution that blocks requests with a User-Agent from outside licensed countries.`, ok: false, why: `User-Agent strings don't show location, so they can't enforce a country rule.` },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "b4-103", mod: 14, domain: "cost", type: "mcq",
    q: `A company runs its main business app on Microsoft SQL Server Standard in an on-premises VM. It wants to use AWS for disaster recovery with an RPO of 30 seconds or less and an RTO of 1 hour. Costs must be kept as low as possible while no disaster is happening. Which solution meets these requirements?`,
    options: [
      { t: `Use AWS Elastic Disaster Recovery to replicate the VM continuously into a low-cost staging area and launch recovery instances when needed.`, ok: true, why: `Continuous block replication gives an RPO of seconds, and recovery instances launch in minutes, while staging uses only small, cheap resources.` },
      { t: `Run a warm standby SQL Server Enterprise instance on EC2 with Always On availability groups and synchronous commit from on premises.`, ok: false, why: `It meets the RPO but pays for a running Enterprise server and license all the time.` },
      { t: `Back up the database to Amazon S3 every hour with AWS Storage Gateway and restore it to RDS for SQL Server during a disaster.`, ok: false, why: `Hourly backups give up to an hour of data loss, which misses the 30-second RPO.` },
      { t: `Take a nightly AMI of the VM with VM Import/Export and launch it on EC2 during a disaster.`, ok: false, why: `A nightly copy loses a full day of data.` },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "b4-104", mod: 10, domain: "performance", type: "mcq",
    q: `A distributor runs an on-premises Oracle database for customer and order data. It wants a managed AWS database that is more available and faster, and it wants to move heavy reporting queries off the primary database. The team wants to avoid rewriting stored procedures and to keep administration to a minimum. Which solution meets these requirements in the <strong>MOST</strong> operationally efficient way?`,
    options: [
      { t: `Migrate to Amazon RDS for Oracle Multi-AZ with AWS DMS, and send reporting queries to an RDS for Oracle read replica.`, ok: true, why: `RDS for Oracle keeps the same engine, Multi-AZ adds high availability, and a read replica carries reporting.` },
      { t: `Convert the schema with AWS SCT, migrate to Amazon Aurora PostgreSQL-Compatible with AWS DMS, and send reporting to Aurora Replicas.`, ok: false, why: `It needs the stored procedures rewritten, which the team wants to avoid.` },
      { t: `Migrate to Oracle on EC2 instances in two Availability Zones with Data Guard, and send reporting to the standby.`, ok: false, why: `It works but the team must run Oracle, Data Guard, patching, and backups itself.` },
      { t: `Migrate to Amazon RDS for Oracle Single-AZ and schedule reports to run at night on the primary.`, ok: false, why: `Single-AZ isn't highly available, and reports still load the primary.` },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "b4-105", mod: 11, domain: "cost", type: "multi",
    q: `A company is building a subscription website. Traffic is unpredictable and can be idle for days. Only users who have paid may sign in and use the app's features. The front end is a static single-page app that calls a REST backend storing user data. Which combination of steps meets these requirements <strong>MOST</strong> cost-effectively? (Choose three.)`,
    options: [
      { t: `Use Amazon API Gateway with AWS Lambda functions that read and write user data in Amazon DynamoDB.`, ok: true, why: `All three bill per request, so idle days cost almost nothing.` },
      { t: `Use an Amazon Cognito user pool to sign in paid users, and an API Gateway Cognito authorizer to protect the API.`, ok: true, why: `Cognito handles sign-in and API Gateway checks tokens, with no auth servers to run.` },
      { t: `Host the single-page app in Amazon S3 and serve it through Amazon CloudFront.`, ok: true, why: `Static hosting on S3 with CloudFront is cheap and needs no servers.` },
      { t: `Run the backend on Amazon ECS with EC2 instances behind an Application Load Balancer, sized for peak traffic.`, ok: false, why: `Instances and the ALB bill every hour, even when the site is idle.` },
      { t: `Use AWS IAM Identity Center to manage the subscribers' sign-in.`, ok: false, why: `Identity Center is for workforce access to AWS, not customer sign-in for an app.` },
      { t: `Host the single-page app on EC2 instances in an Auto Scaling group with a minimum of two instances.`, ok: false, why: `Running instances for static files costs more than S3 and CloudFront.` },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "b4-106", mod: 9, domain: "secure", type: "mcq",
    q: `A music service delivers files and streams from an S3 bucket through Amazon CloudFront. Only paying subscribers may access premium content. Some content is rented for a limited time, such as a single album download valid for 48 hours. The bucket must not be readable directly. Which solution meets these requirements?`,
    options: [
      { t: `Restrict the bucket to CloudFront with origin access control, and give paying users CloudFront signed URLs that expire when the rental ends.`, ok: true, why: `OAC blocks direct S3 access, and signed URLs give per-file, time-limited access.` },
      { t: `Restrict the bucket to CloudFront with origin access control, and add an AWS WAF rule that allows only requests from subscribers' IP addresses.`, ok: false, why: `Subscriber IP addresses change often and don't express time-limited rentals.` },
      { t: `Make the bucket public and give subscribers S3 presigned URLs that bypass CloudFront.`, ok: false, why: `A public bucket can be read by anyone, and it skips the CDN.` },
      { t: `Turn on CloudFront geographic restrictions so that only countries with subscribers can access the distribution.`, ok: false, why: `Geo restriction can't tell subscribers from non-subscribers in the same country.` },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "b4-107", mod: 9, domain: "resilient", type: "mcq",
    q: `A retail company serves public REST APIs through a Regional Amazon API Gateway API with a custom domain name in Route 53. It needs to release a new API version with minimal impact on customers and the ability to roll back quickly if errors rise. Which solution meets these requirements?`,
    options: [
      { t: `Use an API Gateway canary release on the production stage to send a small share of traffic to the new version, then promote it after checks pass.`, ok: true, why: `Canary releases test the new version on a small slice of real traffic and roll back by turning the canary off.` },
      { t: `Deploy the new version to a new stage, then update the custom domain's base path mapping to point all traffic to it at once.`, ok: false, why: `Switching all traffic at once means every customer is hit if the new version has a bug.` },
      { t: `Create a second API in another Region with the new version and use Route 53 weighted routing to move traffic over gradually.`, ok: false, why: `It works but means running a second Regional stack, which is much more work.` },
      { t: `Deploy the new version directly to the production stage during a low-traffic window and monitor errors afterward.`, ok: false, why: `Every user gets the new version right away with no gradual rollout.` },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "b4-108", mod: 9, domain: "resilient", type: "mcq",
    q: `A company's website runs behind an Application Load Balancer, and its DNS is hosted in Route 53. If the site goes down, users must see a static maintenance page instead of a browser error. The company wants minimal changes and no extra servers to run. Which solution meets these requirements?`,
    options: [
      { t: `Set up Route 53 failover routing with a health check on the ALB, and use an S3 static website as the secondary.`, ok: true, why: `When the health check fails, Route 53 points users to the S3 page, which needs no servers.` },
      { t: `Run a second ALB with a fixed-response rule in another Region, and use Route 53 latency-based routing between the two load balancers.`, ok: false, why: `It runs and pays for a second load balancer in another Region, which isn't a minimal change.` },
      { t: `Add a fixed-response rule on the existing ALB listener that returns the maintenance page when targets are unhealthy.`, ok: false, why: `If the ALB or its Region has the problem, the rule can't respond.` },
      { t: `Run a small EC2 instance with a web server that hosts the maintenance page, and switch DNS to it by hand during outages.`, ok: false, why: `It needs a server and a manual DNS change.` },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "b4-109", mod: 14, domain: "cost", type: "mcq",
    q: `A company wants to cut backup costs and stop using physical tapes. Its on-premises backup software writes to a tape library today, and the backup team wants to keep its existing software, jobs, and processes unchanged. Archived tapes are rarely needed after 90 days. What should a solutions architect recommend to meet these requirements?`,
    options: [
      { t: `Deploy AWS Storage Gateway Tape Gateway and connect the backup software to its iSCSI virtual tape library.`, ok: true, why: `The backup software sees a tape library, and tapes are stored in S3 and archived to Glacier.` },
      { t: `Deploy AWS Storage Gateway S3 File Gateway and change the backup jobs to write to an NFS share instead of tapes.`, ok: false, why: `It changes the backup workflow, which the team wants to keep as is.` },
      { t: `Replace the backup software with AWS Backup and protect the on-premises servers directly.`, ok: false, why: `It throws away the existing software and processes.` },
      { t: `Copy each backup to an Amazon EBS volume attached to an EC2 instance that acts as a backup server.`, ok: false, why: `EBS is costly for archives, and the backup jobs would need to change.` },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "b4-110", mod: 8, domain: "performance", type: "mcq",
    q: `An environmental agency has thousands of sensors in the field that stream readings continuously. It needs a platform on AWS that ingests the high-volume stream in near real time, scales without manual shard management, and stores all the data in Amazon S3 for later reports. The team is small. Which solution meets these requirements with the <strong>LEAST</strong> operational overhead?`,
    options: [
      { t: `Use Amazon Data Firehose to ingest the readings and deliver them to Amazon S3.`, ok: true, why: `Firehose is fully managed, scales on its own, and writes straight to S3.` },
      { t: `Use Amazon Kinesis Data Streams in provisioned mode with an AWS Lambda consumer that writes each batch of readings to Amazon S3.`, ok: false, why: `It works but needs shard sizing and consumer code.` },
      { t: `Send readings to an Amazon SQS queue and run EC2 instances that poll the queue and write files to S3.`, ok: false, why: `It means building and running a consumer fleet.` },
      { t: `Run Apache Kafka on EC2 instances and use Kafka Connect to write the topics to S3.`, ok: false, why: `Running Kafka yourself is a lot of operational work.` },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "b4-111", mod: 3, domain: "secure", type: "mcq",
    q: `An online store has an ALB and web tier in public subnets and a MySQL cluster on EC2 instances in private subnets. The database servers must download product and price data from a third-party provider on the internet. The company wants the most secure design that doesn't add management work. What should a solutions architect do to meet these requirements?`,
    options: [
      { t: `Add a NAT gateway in a public subnet and route the private subnets' internet traffic to it.`, ok: true, why: `The servers can reach out while staying unreachable from the internet, and NAT gateways are fully managed.` },
      { t: `Launch a NAT instance in a public subnet from a hardened AMI, disable source/destination checks, and route the private subnets' internet traffic to it.`, ok: false, why: `It works but you must patch, scale, and keep the instance highly available yourself.` },
      { t: `Move the database servers to the public subnets and attach Elastic IP addresses to them.`, ok: false, why: `It exposes the database servers to the internet.` },
      { t: `Add an egress-only internet gateway and route the private subnets' IPv4 traffic to it.`, ok: false, why: `Egress-only internet gateways only handle IPv6 traffic.` },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "b4-112", mod: 11, domain: "secure", type: "multi",
    q: `A team encrypts AWS Lambda environment variables with a customer managed AWS KMS key. When the function runs, it fails to decrypt the variables. A solutions architect must grant the correct permissions so the function can decrypt them while keeping least privilege. Other functions in the account must not gain access to the key. Which steps meet these requirements? (Choose two.)`,
    options: [
      { t: `Add kms:Decrypt for the key to the function's execution role policy.`, ok: true, why: `The function uses its execution role to call KMS.` },
      { t: `Allow the function's execution role to use the key in the KMS key policy.`, ok: true, why: `A customer managed key's policy must allow the role, directly or by delegating to IAM.` },
      { t: `Add kms:Decrypt to the Lambda service's resource-based function policy.`, ok: false, why: `The function policy controls who may invoke the function, not what the function can call.` },
      { t: `Allow the Lambda service principal in the KMS key policy with no conditions.`, ok: false, why: `It grants the whole Lambda service access to the key, which breaks least privilege.` },
      { t: `Grant kms:Decrypt on all keys in the account to the developers' IAM group.`, ok: false, why: `Developers' permissions don't affect what the running function can do.` },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "b4-113", mod: 5, domain: "cost", type: "mcq",
    q: `A bank generates daily reports of about 200 KB each and stores them in Amazon S3. Reports are read often in their first week, then rarely, but must be kept for 7 years. When an auditor asks for an old report, it must be available within 6 hours. Which solution meets these requirements <strong>MOST</strong> cost-effectively?`,
    options: [
      { t: `Store reports in S3 Standard and use a lifecycle rule to move them to S3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval after 7 days.`, ok: true, why: `Glacier Flexible Retrieval's standard retrieval takes 3 to 5 hours, inside the 6-hour limit, at a low price.` },
      { t: `Store reports in S3 Standard and use a lifecycle rule to move them to S3 Glacier Deep Archive after 7 days.`, ok: false, why: `Deep Archive standard retrieval takes up to 12 hours, which misses the 6-hour limit.` },
      { t: `Store reports in S3 Standard and use a lifecycle rule to move them to S3 Standard-IA after 7 days.`, ok: false, why: `It works but costs several times more than Glacier for 7 years of rarely read data.` },
      { t: `Store reports in S3 Intelligent-Tiering from the start without the optional archive tiers.`, ok: false, why: `Without archive tiers, the lowest tier still costs more than Glacier Flexible Retrieval.` },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "b4-114", mod: 14, domain: "cost", type: "mcq",
    q: `A company wants to lower its EC2 bill. Its engineers change the instance families and types of their workloads every few months as they test new generations, and some workloads may move to AWS Fargate. The company doesn't want to pay anything upfront and wants the biggest discount that still allows these changes. Which purchase option meets these requirements?`,
    options: [
      { t: `A 1-year Compute Savings Plan with No Upfront payment.`, ok: true, why: `Compute Savings Plans apply across instance families, Regions, and Fargate, and No Upfront needs no initial payment.` },
      { t: `A 1-year EC2 Instance Savings Plan with No Upfront payment for the current instance family in the Region.`, ok: false, why: `Instance Savings Plans are locked to one family in one Region and don't cover Fargate.` },
      { t: `1-year Standard Reserved Instances with All Upfront payment for the current instance types.`, ok: false, why: `Standard RIs can't change family, and All Upfront needs payment upfront.` },
      { t: `Spot Instances for every workload, with a diversified allocation strategy across instance types.`, ok: false, why: `Not every workload can handle interruptions, so Spot isn't a fit for all of them.` },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "b4-115", mod: 13, domain: "secure", type: "mcq",
    q: `An insurer stores files in Amazon S3 buckets in us-east-1 and us-west-2. A solutions architect must review all of these buckets to find personally identifiable information, such as names, addresses, and passport numbers, without writing custom code or moving data between Regions. Which solution meets these requirements with the <strong>LEAST</strong> operational overhead?`,
    options: [
      { t: `Turn on Amazon Macie in each of the two Regions and run sensitive data discovery jobs on the buckets there.`, ok: true, why: `Macie is a Regional service, so it runs in each Region and scans that Region's buckets with built-in PII identifiers.` },
      { t: `Turn on Amazon Macie in us-east-1 only and add the us-west-2 buckets to its discovery jobs.`, ok: false, why: `Macie jobs only analyze buckets in the Region where Macie runs.` },
      { t: `Replicate the us-west-2 buckets to us-east-1 with S3 Cross-Region Replication, then run Amazon Macie in us-east-1 on all of them.`, ok: false, why: `It moves data between Regions and adds replication cost.` },
      { t: `Use Amazon Athena to query the objects in both Regions with regular expressions that match common PII patterns.`, ok: false, why: `Writing and maintaining PII patterns is custom work that Macie already does.` },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "b4-116", mod: 4, domain: "performance", type: "mcq",
    q: `A manufacturer is moving its SAP application and its SQL Server database from on premises to Amazon EC2. Performance data shows both the application servers and the database servers use a lot of memory, while CPU use stays moderate. The company needs instances that fit this profile. Which solution meets these requirements?`,
    options: [
      { t: `Use memory optimized instances, such as the R or X families, for both the application and the database.`, ok: true, why: `Memory optimized families give a high memory-to-vCPU ratio, which matches both tiers.` },
      { t: `Use compute optimized instances for the application and memory optimized instances for the database.`, ok: false, why: `The application is memory-heavy too, so compute optimized instances would run short on memory.` },
      { t: `Use storage optimized instances for the database and general purpose instances for the application.`, ok: false, why: `Neither choice targets the high memory use seen in both tiers.` },
      { t: `Use burstable T-family instances for both tiers to save cost during quiet periods.`, ok: false, why: `Burstable instances have low memory and CPU credits, which doesn't suit a heavy SAP workload.` },
    ],
  },
]);
